2000:
** ἐπισφαλ-ής**, ές,
(σφάλλομαι) **prone to fall, unstable, precarious,** τὰ μεγάλα πάντα ἐπισφαλῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 497d; -εστέρα δύναμις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1155a10}}; ἐπισφαλές [ἐστι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1264b6; ἐ. φύσει βίος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 126.}}
**II** (σφάλλω) **making to fall, misleading,** εἰς βλάβην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.653d, etc.}}
**II.2** **dangerous,** νόσημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9; νόσοι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.413; καιροί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.66.12}}: _superlative_ -εστάτη, χώρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.29.2; τοῦ ἀγχιβαθοῦς τῶν ἑλῶν Πωμαίοις -σφαλοῦς ἐσομένου 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.2.5}}; ἐπισφαλές [ἐστι] παρακοῦσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 200.}} _adverb_ -λῶς, ἔχειν, διακεῖσθαι, to be **in danger,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.25.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 13; ἐ. βεβηκώς LXX.Wis.4.4}}: _superlative_ -έστατα, περᾶσαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 15.}}
**II.3** **dubious,** _adverb_ _comparative_ -έστερον Aristeas{{Refs|314.}}

2001:
** ἐπισχύω**,
(ἰσχύς) **make strong** or **powerful,** τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.13.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be** or **grow strong,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.1.4}}; **prevail,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.59, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 7}}; **to be urgent,** ἐπίσχυον λέγοντες {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.23.5; ὁ λόγος -ύσει πρὸς συμβουλίαν ἢ διδαχήν 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 48.6.}}

2003:
** ἐπι-τᾰγή**, ἡ,
(ἐπιτάσσω) ={-ταγμα}, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.4.3, LXX.1Esd.1.18; νόμων ἐπιταγαί 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.70; τὰς ἐ. δυσχερῶς φέροντες 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.6.1}}; **imposition** of taxes, αἱ ἐ. τῶν εἰσφορῶν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.19.}}
**2** especially of oracles or divine commands, κατ᾽ ἐπιταγήν {{Refs|“SIG” 1153 (from Athens)}}; κατ᾽ ἐ. τοῦ θεοῦ {{NT|“JHS” 26.28, etc.; κατ᾽ ἐ. τοῦ αἰωνίου θεοῦ NT.Rom.16.26, cf. NT.1Cor.7.6.}}

2004:
** ἐπιτάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐπιτάττω**, **put upon** one **as a duty, enjoin,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 839, etc.}}; τί τινι, as ἐ. ἄεθλόν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43, compare 1.155; ἐπέταξε πόνους ἄλλοισιν ἄλλους 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 9; ἐπιτάξαντος τᾷ πόλει Γαλλίου σῖτον καὶ Ἀνχαρίου ἱμάτια “SIG” 748.25 (1st c.BC)}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **order** one **to** do, ἐ. τοῖσι μὲν πεζὸν στρατὸν..παρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.83, compare 3.159, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 69, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.11, etc.}}: rarely with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **enjoin** or **order that..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.8}}; with the case omitted, ἐ. ἀποφορὴν ἐπιτελέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109, compare 137}}: _absolutely_, **impose commands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140, +others}}; τινί on one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 664}}:— _passive_, **accept orders, submit to commands,** εἰ 'πιταξόμεσθα δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 521; ἐπιταττόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 686}}: with _infinitive_, οἱ ἐπιταττόμενοι γαμεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 925e: with _accusative_ _of things_, ἄλλο τι ἐπιταχθήσεσθε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140}}; of things, **to be ordered,** ὁ στρατὸς ὁ -θεὶς ἑκάστοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95; so Λακεδαιμονίοις..ναῦς ἐπετάχθησαν ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7}} assuming variant; τὰ ἐπιτασσόμενα ἐπετέλεον **orders given,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115; τἀπιταχθέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 20b, +others; κατὰ νόμον τὸν ἐπιταχθησόμενον [prev. author] “Lg.” 740c}}; δικαίωσις τοῖς πέλας -ομένη **dictated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141: in Mathematics texts, τὸ ἐπιταχθέν}} what was **prescribed,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 4.1, +others}}; πλευρὰς ἔχον ὅσας ἄν τις ἐπιτάξῃ with as many sides as you **please,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 290.26.}}
**2** **use the imperative mood,** εὔχεσθαι οἰόμενος ἐπιτάττει εἰπὼν μῆνιν ἄειδε θεά.. τὸ γὰρ κελεῦσαι, φησί,..ἐπίταξίς ἐστιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b16}}; opposed to κελεύειν, {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.33.}}
**II** **place next** or **beside,** [Σαγάρτιοι] ἐπετετάχατο ἐς τοὺς Πέρσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.85; ἐπετέτακτο Ἀριστοκράτει Περικλῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.29:}} —_middle_, τοὺς ἱππέας ἐπετάξαντο ἐπὶ τῷ δεξιῷ they **had** the cavalry **placed next,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.67.}}
**II.2** **place behind,** ὄπισθεν τοῦ πεζοῦ τὴν ἵππον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 471d (_passive_)}}:—_middle_, ἐπιτάξασθαι τῇ φάλαγγι λόχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.9}}:—_passive_, τοῖσι μυρίοισι ἐπετέτακτο ἵππος Περσέων μυρίη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.41, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 31, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 29.8, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 25.10; Ἀράβιοι ἔσχατοι ἐπετετάχατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.87.}}
**II.2.b** Grammars, **place after,** in _passive_, αὐτὸς πάσῃ ἀντωνυμίᾳ -τάσσεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 34.10, compare “Synt.” 138.23.}}
**II.3** **set in command over,** τινί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.24.1:}} —_passive_, οἱ ἐπιτεταγμένοι **set as guards over** the wagons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.72; ταῖς βασιλικαῖς ἐπιστολαῖς -ταχθείς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.24.1, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.63d.}}

2005:
** ἐπιτελ-έω**,
_future_ **-τελῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 229.17 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC)}}, _Doric dialect3rd.pers._ _plural_ _future_ **-τελεσσεῦντι**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Annuario” 4 /5.225.27 (Rhodes, 2nd c.BC)}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _perfect_ **-τετελέκαντι**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1158.3 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}:—**complete, finish, accomplish,** ἐ. τὰ ἐπιτασσόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, compare 51, 90; τὰς ἐντολάς [prev. work] 157}}; τὸν προκείμενον ἄεθλον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 126; ἀποδείξιας 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 4; ἐ. ἔργῳ ἃ ἂν γνῶσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70; ταῦτα τοῖς ἔργοις ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.38; πόλεμον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.65.2}}; especially of **the fulfilment** of oracles, visions, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13 (_passive_), +others}}; εὐχήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 86; ἃ ὑπέσχετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138:}} —_middle_, τὴν κρίσιν ἐπιτελέσασθαι **get** it **completed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 27c; καλὴν καὶ σεμνὴν πρᾶξιν -τετελεσμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.22.1:}} —_passive_, ὅπως ἂν ἡ εἰρήνη ἐπιτελεσθῇ that it **may be brought to pass,** Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.29}}; of movements, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 19.5}}; [παθήματα] τῇ ἀδελφότητι ἐ.{{NT|NT.1Pet.5.9.}}
**2** **bring to perfection,** τὴν γένεσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 741b5, compare “HA” 539a33}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 758b26.}}
**3** _passive_ in Logic, of a syllogism, **to be made perfect,** by reduction to the first figure, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 28a5, 41b4.}}
**II** **discharge** a religious duty, θυσίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.16, “Inscription Prien.” 108.27 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ νομιζόμενα τοῖς θεοῖς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.35.50 (2nd c.BC)}}; νηστείας καὶ ὁρτάς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.186; λατρείας NT.Heb.9.6}}(so in _middle_, εὐωχίαν ἐπετελέσατο {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 113.61 (1st c.BC))}}: _absolutely_, **sacrifice,** τινί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.61.}}
**II.2** **celebrate,** τὴν τοῦ Κυνὸς ἐ. ἐπιτολήν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 113.14.}}
**III** **pay in full,** ἀποφορήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109; πεντακόσια τάλαντα βασιλέϊ τὸν ἐπέτειον φόρον [prev. author] 5.49, compare 82, 84; ἐπιμήνια [prev. author] 8.41: _metaphorically_ in}}_middle_, ἐπιτελεῖσθαι τὰ τοῦ γήρως **to have to pay, be subject to,** the burdens of old age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.8}}; ἐ. θάνατον **have to pay the debt of** death, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 33:}} —_passive_, ἡ δίκη.. τοῦ φόνου.. ἐκ Μαρδονίου ἐπετελέετο **was paid in full** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.64.}}
**IV** **impose upon,** ἀσεβείας δίκην τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 910d.}}

2006:
** ἐπιτήδ-ειος**, α, ον:
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐπιτήδ-εος**, έη, εον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.158, +others (compare ἐπιταδεοτρώκτας)}}: _Doric dialect_ **-τάδειος** [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 524.36 (Praesus, 3rd c.BC): regular _comparative_ and _superlative_ -ειότερος, -ειότατος, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54, 7.86, etc.}}; -έστερος, -έστατος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see entry , 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 121: _Ionic dialect_ -εότερος, -εότατος, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.2, 1.110, +others}}: (ἐπιτηδές):— **made for an end** or **purpose, fit** or **adapted for** **it, suitable, convenient,** νομαί {{Refs|[prev. passage], etc.:—Constr.: ἐ. ἔς τι [prev. work] 115 (_superlative_)}}, etc.; πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 390b: with}}_infinitive_, χωρίον -ότατον ἐνιππεῦσαι **most fit** to ride in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.102, compare 9.2 (_comparative_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1228, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 508; ἄνδρα -ότατον..δέξαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 79; so ἐ. τῷ σώματι κινδυνεύειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.63}}; ἐ. ὑπεξαιρεθῆναι **convenient** to be put out of the way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.70; τεθνάναι μᾶλλον ἢ σῴζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.25, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.24}}; ἐ. ξυνεῖναι a **fit** person to live with, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 206}}; also ἐ. ὀστρακισθῆναι **deserving** to be ostracized, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.36; ἐ. πάσχειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.57}}; ἐκλεγόμενος τὸν ἐ. ἔπαισεν ἄν struck **him who deserved it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11}}; but ἐ. ἐς ὀλιγαρχίαν ἐλθεῖν **likely** or **inclined** to come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.63; also ὑμῖν ἐπιτήδεόν [ἐστι] οἰκέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.158, etc.}}
**II** **useful, serviceable, necessary,**
**II.1** of things, ὀλιγαρχία ἐ. τοῖς Λακεδαιμονίοις **fit** or **serviceable for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.81; ἐ. τῷ δήμῳ πράττειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.51}}; καταστήσειν ἐς τὸ ἐ. to their **advantage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.76}}; οὐδὲν ηὕροντο ἐ. no **advantage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.58; οὐκ ἐ. καταγνῶναί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97}}; ἱερὰ οὐκ ἐ., opposed to καλά, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.37: especially as}} _substantive_, τὰ ἐ. things **requisite, necessaries,** especially of **provisions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.174, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.2, etc.}}: also in _singular_, **what is requisite, needful,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Vect.” 4.38.}}
**II.2** of persons, **serviceable, friendly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72 (_superlative_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}; τινί to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.78, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.14; ἐ. ποιεῖν τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.41}}; ἐ. τῷ πατρί **conformable to** his **will,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}; ἐ. τοῖς πρασσομένοις **favourable** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.54}}: also as _substantive_, **a close friend,** οἱ ἐ. one's **friends,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.64; Ἀθηναίων ἐ. [prev. author] 7.73; μοι ἐ. καὶ φίλος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.22.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, ={ἄξιος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1073.19 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD).}}
**III** _adverb_ -είως, _Ionic dialect_ -έως, **studiously, carefully,** ὑπηρετέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 4.139.}}
**III.2** **suitably, conveniently, fitly,** ποιέειν ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.7.β᾽}}; ἐ. σφίσιν αὐτοῖς πολιτεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19; ἐ. ἔχειν [prev. author] 5.82}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.54; -οτέρως, διαιτᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.32.}}
**III.3** ἐ. ἔχειν τινί to be **on friendly terms** with.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.9.3.}}

2007:
** ἐπιτίθημι**,
_passive_ mostly furnished by ἐπίκειμαι: _active_, **lay, put** or **place upon,** of offerings **laid on** the altar, ἐπὶ μηρία θέντες Ἀπόλλωνι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.267, compare 3.179; λιβανωτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 426, “V.” 96, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18}}; **set** meats **on** the table, εἴδατα πόλλ᾽ ἐπιθεῖσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.140, compare 10.355}}; πάντ᾽ ἐπιθεῖτε **on** the car, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.264; [νέκυας] ἐπὶ νηυσὶ τιθέντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.419}}; τινὶ κύρτον καὶ κώπαν, as a grave-monument, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.505 (= 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 120): Constr. mostly ἐ. τινί τι, τῷ ἰσχυροτέρῳ πλέον βάρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.9, etc.}}: but also c._genitive_, ἐ. λεχέων τινά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.589; ἐ. τι ἐπί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ᾽}}; κεφαλὴν ἐπὶ στέρνα τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.14}}: with _accusative_ only, **put upon, set up,** ἐ. φάρμακα **apply** salves, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.190; δέελον δ᾽ ἐπὶ σῆμά τ᾽ ἔθηκε 10.466; στήλην λίθου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.183; φάκελον ξύλων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 243}}; ἐ. μνημεῖά τινι to him, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 702, compare “IG” 14.446 (from Tauromenium), 12.1068.}}
**2** **set upon, turn towards,** Ἑκτορέοις ἐπὶ φρένα θῆχ᾽ ἱεροῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.46}}; but τῇ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐπὶ φρεσὶ θῆκε with _infinitive_, **put** it **into** her mind to.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.1.}}
**II** **put on** a covering or lid, ὡς εἴ τε φαρέτρῃ πῶμ᾽ ἐπιθείη [{{Refs|9.314; κεφαλῇ ἐπέθηκε (as variant for{ἐφύπερθἐ καλύπτρην} 5.232}}]; λίθον δ᾽ ἐπέθηκε θύρῃσι, i.e. **put** a stone **as a door** to the cave, **put** it **before** the door, {{Refs|13.370}}; also, **put** a door **to,** κολλητὰς ἐπέθηκα θύρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.194; θύρας ἐπέθηκε φαεινάς 21.45; θυρεὸν μέγαν 9.240 (see. below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11).}}
**II.2** **set** a seal **on**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 361 iii 22 (2nd c.AD)}}; **apply** a pessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 214 (_passive_)}}; a cupping instrument, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.11 (_passive_).}}
**III** **put to, add, grant** or **give besides,** ὅσσα τε νῦν ὔμμ᾽ ἐστὶ καὶ εἴ ποθεν ἄλλ᾽ ἐπιθεῖτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.62, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.364, etc.; κράτος, κῦδός τινι, 1.509 (tmesis), 23.400 (tmesis), 406 (tmesis); ἡμιτάλαντον χρυσοῦ [prev. work] 796.}}
**III.2** of Time, **add, bring on,** ἕβδομον ἦμαρ ἐπὶ Ζεὺς θῆκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.399; μάλα πολλὰ [ἔτεα] 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 697.}}
**IV** **put on** as a finish, χρυσέην ἐπέθηκε κορώνην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.111; περόνην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.256: _metaphorically_, οὐδὲ τέλος μύθῳ ἐπιθήσεις}} **add** fulfilment, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.107, compare 20.369; so later ἐ. κεφάλαἰ ἐφ᾽ ἅπασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.18; κολοφῶνα ἐ. τῇ σοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 301e}}; τέλος ἐπιτεθήκατον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 272a; πέρας ἐ. τῇ γενέσει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 776a4; πίστιν ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.22, 49.42; ὁ δὲ μισθωσάμενος πίστιν ἐπιθήσει πρὸς τοὺς νεωποίας “SIG” 963.34 (Arcesine, 4th c.BC)}}; πέρας ἐ. τῷ πράγματι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 25.7 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; ὅρον ἐ. τῷ πράγματι Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 87.2 (2nd c.AD).}}
**V** **impose, inflict** a penalty, σοὶ δέ, γέρον, θωὴν ἐπιθήσομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.192}}; δίκην, ζημίην, ἄποινα ἐ. τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 144, 9.120, etc.; θάνατον δίκην ἐ. τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 838c; δίκην τὴν πρέπουσαν [prev. author] “Criti.” 106b; ἔργων ἀντ᾽ ἀδίκων χαλεπὴν ἐ. ἀμοιβήν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 334; τιμωρίαν ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.11 (compare below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV)}}: so of burdens, grievances, etc., θήσειν..ἐπ᾽ ἄλγεα Τρωσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.39; οἷσιν ἐπὶ Ζεὺς θῆκε κακὸν μόρον 6.357; [ἄτην] οἱ ἐπὶ φρεσὶθῆκε..Ἐρινύς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.234}}; ἀνάγκην ἐ. with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 10.7}}; ἐ...μὴ τυγχάνειν **imposing as a penalty** not to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.3 (see. below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV).}}
**VI** **dispatch** a letter, ἐ. τι ἐς Αἴγυπτον, ἐς Μυτιλήνην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 5.95; ἐ. [ἐπιστολάς] 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.28.}}
**VII** **give** a name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 205b, etc.}}
**VIII** **contribute** (capital) **to** a venture, ἐς πεῖραν {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 9.44.}}
**B** _middle_, with _perfect_ _passive_ ἐπιτέθειμαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.975d}} also _aorist_ _passive_, {{Refs|“Inscription Prien.” (see. below)}}, etc.:—**put on oneself** or **for oneself,** ἐπὶ στεφάνην κεφαλῆφιν..θήκατο **placed** a helmet **on his** head, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.30; κρατὶ δ᾽ ἐπὶ..κυνέην θέτο 5.743, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 702 (tmesis), etc.}}; χεῖρας ἐπ᾽ ἀνδροφόνους θέμενος στήθεσσι **laying one's** hands **upon.**., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.317}}; κτύπημα χειρὸς κάρᾳ **on one's** head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1210 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II** **put on** or **to,** as a door, πύλας τοῖς ὠσὶν ἐπίθεσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b; θύρας 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 245, +others, etc. (see. above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11).}}
**B.III** **apply oneself to, employ oneself on** or **in,** with _dative_, ναυτιλίῃσι μακρῇσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1}}; τῇ πείρᾳ, τοῖς ἔργοις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.3, etc.; τοῖς πολιτικοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527d}}: with _infinitive_, **attempt to.**., φιλοσοφεῖν ἐπέθετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 36.3; γράφειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242b}}:—_passive_, ἐπετέθη πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 17.38 (3rd c.BC).}}
**B.III.2** **make an attempt upon, attack,** τῇ Εὐβοίῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.31; Ἐφεσίοισι [prev. author] 1.26, compare 102, 8.27; τῷ δήμῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61; τῇ δημοκρατίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.12}}; ἐ. τῇ τοῦ δήμου καταλύσει **attempt** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.235; τυραννίδι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 125; ἀρχῇ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.772d}}; ἐ. ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις or τοῖς ἀτυχήμασί τινος **take advantage of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.70}}: _absolutely_, **make an attack,** κατ᾽ ἀμφότερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302b25.}}
**B.III.3** _absolutely_, δικαιοσύνην ἐπιθέμενος ἤσκεε he practised justice **with assiduity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, compare 6.60.}}
**B.IV** **bring on oneself,** ἐπέθου θύος δημοθρόους τ᾽ ἀράς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1409.}}
**B.IV.2** **cause** a penalty **to be imposed,** θάνατον ζημίαν ἐπιθέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.24; φόβον τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.41.}}
**B.V** **lay commands on,** τί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111, compare “OGI” 669.61 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}: also with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.63, variant in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.465d.}}
**B.VI** **give** a name, τινί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.554 (tmesis), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451b10.}}
**B.VII** **contribute,** πολλοὶ ἐπέθεντο τὰς ἐπιδόσεις εἰς τὴν παρασκευὴν τοῦ πολέμου probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 346.29 (4th c.BC).}}

2008:
** ἐπιτιμ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and Delph. ἐπιτιμέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39, “Schwyzer” 346.11 (2nd c.BC)}}:— **lay a value upon**: hence,
**1** **show honour to,** τινά Hdt. [prev. cited]
**2** **raise in** **price,** οἶνον ἐ. πολύ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.27: _absolutely_, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.50}}; τὴν αἴτησιν ἐ. **raise** the demand, {{Refs|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}:—_passive_, **rise in price,** of corn, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.39, 50.6, “PSI” 4.356.7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** of judges, **lay a penalty on** a person, τοῖς ἐξάρνοις ἐ. ταλάντου ἑκάστῳ variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.113; ἐ. ἀργύριον “Hermes” 17.4 (from Delos)}}; but ἐ. τὴν ἀρχαίην δίκην **make** the original trial **the ground of punishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43.}}
**II.2** **object to** one **as blameable,** τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237c, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.17, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455a26.}}
**II.2.b** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **censure,** οὐ τοῦτ᾽ ἐπιτιμῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.148, compare 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 49}}:—_passive_, τὸ..ὑπὸ τῶν πολλῶν ἐπιτιμώμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1114a29.}}
**II.2.c** with _dative_ only, **rebuke, censure,** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.17}}; of things, τοῖς ψηφισθεῖσιν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.52; τοῖς πεπραγμένοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.64; τοῖς ἀνέμοις NT.Matt.8.26; τινὶ περί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.9.1; τινὶ ὡς.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.7; τῷ λόγῳ, ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169d}}:— _passive_, ἐπιτετιμημένος ἐπί τινι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.12.9.}}
**II.2.d** _absolutely_, λόγῳ καλῶς ἐ. by word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, compare 4.28; τὸ μὲν ἐπιτιμᾶν..φήσαι τις ἂν ῥᾴδιον εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a27.}}

2009:
** ἐπιτιμ-ία**, ἡ,
**the condition of an** ἐπίτιμος, **enjoyment of all civilrights and privileges,** opposed to ἀτιμία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.88, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.106}}; τὸ συνειλεγμένον εἰς τὴν ἐ. money collected for the **recovery of the franchise,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.312; ἡ ἐ. σου οὐδὲν βλαβήσεται “POxy.” 1405.10 (3rd c.AD),compare “Schwyzer” 328.11 (Delph., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **punishment, penalty,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.3.10, “OGI” 669.43 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**III** **dignity, respect,** ἀξιώματος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.45}}; **good name,** πάντα ποιεῖν ὑπὲρ τῆς ἰδίας ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.312.}}

2010:
** ἐπιτρέπ-ω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐπιτράπω** [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81:}}_future_ -τρέψ; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ **-τρέψοντι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.21}}; Cretan dialect _infinitive_ **-τραψῆν**{{Refs|“GDI” 5039.21,5024.12}}: _aorist_ I **-έτρεψα**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.116, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ -έτραπον{{Refs|[prev. work] 59}}: _perfect_ **-τέτρᾰφα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.6.6:}} —_middle_, _future_ -τρέψομαι (variant{-τράψ-}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155}}: _aorist 2_ **-ετρᾰπόμην**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.12:}} —_passive_, _future_ -τετράψομαι Pisistr. cited in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.54}}: _aorist_ I **-ετρέφθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.5}}; _Ionic dialect_ -ετράφθην, _participle_ **-τραφθείς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7}}: _aorist 2_ -ετράπην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31}}: _perfect_ (see. below {{Refs|1.6)}}:—properly **to turn to** or **towards,** used by Hom.in _aorist 2_ _middle_, σοὶ.. θυμὸς ἐπετράπετο εἴρεσθαι thy mind **inclined itself** to ask, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.12.}}
**b** **to overturn upon,** τινί τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 8.}}
**2** **turn over to, transfer, bequeath,** παισὶν ἐπιτρέψειεν ἕκαστος κτήματ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάροισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.149.}}
**3** **commit, entrust to** another as trustee, guardian, or vicegerent, οἱ.. ἐπέτρεπεν οἶκον ἅπαντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.226; ἐπιτρέψειας ἕκαστα δμῳάων [ἐκείνῃ] ἥ τις..ἀρίστη 15.24, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.509}}; θεοῖσι μῦθον ἐπιτρέψαι **leave** it to them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.289, compare 19.502}}; so κάκοισι θῦμον ἐπιτρέπην (_Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 35.1; σμικραῖς ἑαυτοὺς ἐ. ἐλπίσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 921}}; frequently in Prose, ἐ. τινὶ τὰ πρήγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.26; τὴν πόλιν [prev. author] 4.202; Νάξον Λυγδάμι [prev. author] 1.64; τὰ πάντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; πλεῖστα τῷ ἀλογίστῳ [prev. author] 5.99; τὴν ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.31, etc.}}; also a son for education, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 200d}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, τινά τινι γερονταγωγεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1098}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_, **refer** a legal issue to any one, τινὶ δίαιταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.45; διάγνωσις -τετράφθω τῷ ἐπιμελητῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936a; οἷς (attracted for ἃ) ἂν ἐπιτρέψωσιν οἱ δὲ τάξωσι, τούτοις ἐμμένειν, i.e. acquiesce in the court and abide by its decision, [prev. work] 784c}} (for the _construction_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.34 near the end).}}
**4** with _dative_ only, **rely upon, leave to,** τοῖσιν γὰρ ἐπετράπομέν γε μάλιστα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.59; ἐπιτρέψαι δὲ θεοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.279; ἐ. τῇ ὀλιγαρχίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81; ὥς οἱ (i.e. ἰατρῷ) ἐπέτρεψε [prev. work] 130: with _dative_ et}}_infinitive_, σοὶ ἐπέτρεψεν πονέεσθαι he **left** it to you to work, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.116, compare 421, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.10}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_, **refer the matter** to a person, **leave it to his arbitration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1115, “V.” 521, “Ra.” 811}}; τινὶ δικαστῇ to one as a judge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.83; τῷ ἐν Δελφοῖς μαντείῳ [prev. author] 1.28; ἐ. τῷ θεῷ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512e, cf. Alcaeus1.117e; ὑμῖν ἐπιτρέπω καὶ τῷ θεῷ κρῖναι [prev. author] “Ap.” 35d}}; Ἀθηναίοις ἐ. περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν πλὴν θανάτου **to leave** their case to the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} save as to the penalty of death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54; περὶ ὧν διαφερόμεθα τοῖς οἰκείοις ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.1}}:—_passive_, δίκης Λακεδαιμονίοις ἐπιτραπείσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31.}}
**5** _middle_, **entrust oneself, leave one's case to,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96; διαιτητῇ [prev. author] 5.95, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8}}; also, **to entrust what is one's own to** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155, 157.}}
**6** _passive_, **to be entrusted,** ᾧ λαοί τ᾽ ἐπιτετράφαται (_3rd.pers._ _plural_ _perfect_ for ἐπιτετραμμένοι εἰσί) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.25}}; τῇς (i.e. Ὥραις) ἐπιτέτραπται μέγας οὐρανός heaven's gate **is committed to** them (to open and to shut),{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.750, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142}}; ὑπό τινων ἐπιτρεφθῆναι (i.e. ἰατρῷ), of a patient, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.5:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, ἐπιτρέπομαί τι I **am entrusted with** a thing, ἐπιτραφθέντες τὴν ἀρχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7; ἐπιτετραμμένοι τὴν φυλακήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126.}}
**II** **give up, yield,** Ποσειδάωνι δὲ νίκην πᾶσαν ἐπέτρεψας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.473}}; later ἐ. τινί with _infinitive_, **permit, suffer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1078, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 171e, etc.}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.8; also ἐ. Θηβαίοις αὐτονόμους εἶναι [prev. author] “HG” 6.3.9; οὐδενὶ ἐ. κακῷ εἶναι [prev. author] “An.” 3.2.31; ἐ. ἀδικέοντι τῷ ἀδελφεῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120; μὴ ἐ. τῷ ἀσεβοῦντι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5e}}: _absolutely_, **give way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 799, “Pl.” 915, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35b:}} — _passive_, ἄνευ τοῦ ἐπιτραπῆναι without **leave,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 474.40 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** _intransitive_, **give way,** οὐ μὲν ἐπέτρεπε γήραϊ λυγρῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.79}}; **indulge,** μὴ πάντα ἡλικίῃ καὶ θυμῷ ἐπίτρεπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36; ταις ἡδοναῖς καὶ ἐπιθυμίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802c; τῇ ὀργῇ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.45.}}
**III** **command,** τὴν μὲν [τάξιν] ἐπὶ τῷ δεξιῷ ἐπέτρεψεν ἐφέπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.11: elsewhere with _dative_, “PLond.” 3.1173.3 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—_passive_, ἐπετράπην ὑπὸ σοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 51.5 (2nd c.AD).}}

2011:
** ἐπιτροπ-ή**, ἡ,
**reference,** especially to an arbiter in decision of a law-suit, ἠξίουν δίκης ἐπιτροπὴν σφίσι γενέσθαι ἢ ἐς πόλιν τινὰ ἢ ἰδιώτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.41}}; ἡ ἐ. τούτῳ πρὸς Παρμένοντα γέγονε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.23; εἰς ἐ. ἔρχεσθαι [prev. work]14; ἡ ἐ. ἐγένετό μοι [prev. work]16; τὴν ἐ. λῦσαι [prev. passage]; ἀνέντες τὴν ἐ.}} having declined it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31.}}
**2** generally, **power to decide,** ἐ. διδόναι τινὶ περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 17, compare “Schwyzer” 195.10 (Crete (from Delos), 2nd c.BC)}}; διδόναι τῇ συγκλήτῳ τὴν ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.39.5}}; διδόναι ἑαυτοὺς εἰς ἐ., or τὴν ἐ. διδόναι περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν, Latin _dedere se in fidem,_ to surrender absolutely, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.11.8, 15.8.14, etc.}}; ἐ. λαβεῖν εἰς τὸ διαλῦσαι to receive **full powers** to treat, {{NT+1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.15.7, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.45, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.47; μετ᾽ ἐξουσίας καὶ ἐ. NT.Act.26.12.}}
**II** **guardianship,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 924b, etc.}}; ἐπιτροπῆς κατάστασις, διαδικασία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.6; ἀποχὴ τῆς ἐ. “POxy.” 898.24 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπιτροπῆς δικάζεσθαι, of an action brought by a ward against a guardian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 27; καταγιγνώσκειν τὴν ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.58; ἐπιτροπῆς κρίνειν τινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.844c.}}
**II.2** **office of a Roman procurator,** ἡ τοῦ ἰδίου λόγου ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 16.8 (2nd c.AD)}}: generally, **stewardship,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PLond. 2.454.10 (4th c.AD).}}

2012:
** ἐπίτροπ-ος**, ον,
(ἐπιτρέπω) **one to whom the charge of anything is entrusted, steward, trustee, administrator,** c._genitive_ _of things_, τῶν ἑωυτοῦ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108; τῶν οἰκίων [prev. author] 3.63: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.78, 27.19, NT.Luke.8.3, etc.}}; **steward, messman,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.35: _metaphorically_, τῶν [τοῦ Πρωταγόρου] ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165a.}}
**2** = Latin _procurator,_ Καίσαρος ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.4.20, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.813e, etc.}}; ἐ. Σεβαστοῦ, -τῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OG” 1502.10 (Aezani, 2nd c.AD), 501.2 (Tralles, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἐ. τῆς Ἠπείρου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.4.1; τῶν μετάλλων “OG” 1678.5 (Egypt, 2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** **governor, viceroy,** οἱ ἐ. τῆς Μέμφιος, Μιλήτου ἐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.27, 5.30, compare 106.}}
**4** **executor,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.9, +others(3rd c.BC).}}
**II** c._genitive_ _person_, **trustee, guardian,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.80, etc.; ἐ. τινι παίδων 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 42: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 924b, etc.; ὑπὸ ἐπιτρόπους εἶναι NT.Gal.4.2; καθιστάναι ἐ. “PRyl.” 153.18 (2nd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, **guardian, protector,** θεὸς ἐ. ἐών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.106.}}

2013:
** ἐπιτυγχάνω**,
_future_ **-τεύξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431c:}}_aorist 2_ ἐπέτῠχον: _perfect_ **-τετύχηκα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352b5}}:—properly **hit the mark,** τοῦ σκοποῦ, opposed to ἀποτυγχάνω, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “EN” 1106b33; οἱ πολλὰ βάλλοντες ἐπιτυγχάνουσι πολλάκις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.438a: hence}}
**II** **light** or **fall upon, meet with,**
**I** with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 195, 535, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75; ἐ. γυναικὶ βιαζομένῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 874c: also c._dative_ _of things_, ἐ. σορῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68; ναυσί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.34; βιβλίῳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 27}}; ἐ.[ταῖς θύραις] ἀνεῳγμέναις **to find** them open, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223b.}}
**I.2** c._genitive_ _person_, μετρίου ἀνδρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 245, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 12: c._genitive_ _of things_, ἐ. ὁλκάδος ἀναγομένης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3}}; εὐώνων ἐ. a low market, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oec.” [prev. cited]}}
**I.3** rarely c._accusative_, τὰς ἁπλᾶς [ἐπιθυμίας] ἐν ὀλίγοις ἐπιτεύξῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431c; ἅττ᾽ ἂν ἐπιτύχῃς 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 123.5.}}
**I.4** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 570, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.38}}; mostly ὁ ἐπιτυχών **the first person one meets, any chance person,** especially in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1: with _negative_, οὐδὲ φαύλων ἀνδρῶν οὐδὲ τῶν ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 390d; οὐ γὰρ οἶμαι τοῦ ἐ. εἶναι.. [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 4a}}; οὐ περὶ τοῦ ἐ. on no **common matter,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 352d}}: without the _Article_, ἐπιτυχόντος ἀνθρώπου λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1248, cf. Phot. p.140R. (=gloss on 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 25D.).}}
**III** **attain to, reach, gain** one's end, c._genitive_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.3; εὐχωλᾶς “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 134 H.(4th c.BC)}}; πολιτείας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 113.3 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τοῦ καλῶς [μειγνύναι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61d}}; ἐ. τοῦ ἀγῶνος **gain** one's suit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.30}}; **profit by, benefit by,** φιλανθρωπίας {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 522.8 (2nd c.AD): _absolutely_, οὐ δύνασθε ἐπιτυχεῖν NT.Jam.4.2.}}
**III.2** c._participle_, **succeed in** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.101, 103 (but ζητέων ἐπιτυγχάνειν}} **find, light upon** by searching, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 24)}}: so with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 6}}; γαμβροῦ ὁ ἐπιτυχὼν εὗρεν υἱόν he who **is lucky in** his son-in-law, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 272.}}
**III.3** c._dative_ modi, **to be lucky, successful in** a thing, μάχῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.165}}: _absolutely_, **to be successful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97c, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a9}}; ἂν ἐπιτύχῃ if she **succeeds,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 346; τἆλλα ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.19: also _impersonal_, αὐτῷ οὐδὲν ἐπετύγχανε 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 41.6.}}
**III.4** _passive_, **turn out well,** αἱ ἐπιτετευγμέναι πράξεις **successful,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.2, compare 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.44.81, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.674a}}; φάρμακον -τετευγμένον **proved** remedy, Heraclid._Tarentum dialect_ cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.403.}}
**IV** c._dative_ _person_, **converse, talk with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 758c.}}

2014:
** ἐπιφαίνω**,
_aorist_ I (late) **-έφᾱνα**{{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.33.2, NT.Luke.1.79}}:—**show forth, display,** _absolutely_, μηδὲ λίην ἐπίφαινε {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 359 (assuming variant); ἐ.προστασίαν ἀξιωματικήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.18.8; μηδὲν τεχνικόν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.10}}; τὸ ἀγέρωχον, τὴν προαίρεσιν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 1, 2.139d; ἀνθρωπόμορφόν τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 12}}; τὸν μισοπόνηρον **play** the μ., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.74 W}}.:—_passive_, **come into view,** ἠέλιος δ᾽ ἐπέλαμψε, μάχη δ᾽ ἐπὶ πᾶσα φαάνθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.650}}; of an enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152, 4.122, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.42, etc.}}; διὰ τὸ ἐπιφανέντα με κωλῦσαι Test. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.22; ἐ.ἐς τὴν Νάξον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.13; ἐπιφανῆναι ἐπὶ τὸ ἔργον [prev. author] “Oec.” 21.10; ἐπὶ τὰ Δρέπανα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.49.7; ἐ.τινὶ ἐς οἶκον}}**to present oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97}}; ἐ. τινί **show oneself, appear** to one, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.24, +others}}; frequently of dreams and visions, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.91, 3.27; ἐν τῷ ὕπνῳ [prev. author] 7.16}}; of a divine manifestation, ἐπιφαινομένης αὐτοῖς Ἀρτέμιδος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 557.5 (Magn.Mae., 3rd c.BC),compare “IG” 4.951.26 (Epid., 3rd c.BC)}}; so ἡ χρηστότης ἐπεφάνη τοῦ θεοῦ {{NT|NT.Tit.3.4}}; of an Emperor, δήμοις ἑορτάζουσιν ἐπιφανείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.7.2; also ἵνα σφι τιμωρίη..ἐπιφανήσεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.49; τὰ ἐπιφαινόμενα}}**symptoms which make their appearance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.12, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.17, +others}}
**2** c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, **make it manifest that..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1044d.}}
**3** _passive_, literal **appear upon the surface,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101d; ἐ. ταῖς εἰκόσιν ἰδέα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 3: _metaphorically_, τὸ ἦθος ὡς χρῶμα ταῖς πράξεσιν ἐ. [prev. work] 48,compare “Galb.” 23.}}
**II** seemingly _intransitive_, in _active_, **show light, dawn,** ἡμέρας ἐπιφαινούσης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.6.6 (so ἀνατολῆς ἐπιφαινομένης [prev. author] 3.113.1); ἐπιφᾶναι τοῖς ἐν σκότει καθημένοις}}**to shine upon** {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.1.79, cf. LXX.Deu.33.2.}}

2016:
** ἐπιφαν-ής**, ές,
**coming to light, coming suddenly into view, appearing,** of gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.27, etc.}}: hence, **present to aid,** θεοὶ -έστατοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.17.}}
**2** of places and things, **in full view,** πόλις ἐ. ἔξωθεν, of a place **commanded by another,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10, compare 6.96, 7.19}}; τινι **to** one,{{Refs|[prev. work] 3}}; ἔχειν ἐπιφανεῖς θηλάς **visible,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 504b23}}; φλέβες **prominent,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).209.}}
**3** **manifest, evident,** ὄνειδος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 218 (_comparative_)}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐπιφανεστάτων σημείων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21; διὰ τὸ μὴ ἐ. εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126a23.}}
**II** of men, **conspicuous, notable, distinguished** by rank, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, +others; οἰκίη οὐκ ἐ. [prev. work] 172}}; **notable,** either for good or ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.10, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.12 (_superlative_)}}; ἀνδρείᾳ **for** courage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72; πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 629e}}; **famous, renowned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.6 (_comparative_), etc.}}; ἀνδρῶν ἐ. πᾶσα γῆ τάφος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43}}; of things, places, etc., χώρα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.46 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; -εστέρα τιμή {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IPE” 12.34.22 (Olbia, 1st c.BC)}}; -έσταται τιμαί {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).1109.10 (Magn.Thess.).}}
**II.2** of things, **remarkable,** οὗτοί σφεων οἱ -έστατοι νόμοι εἰσί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6; -εστάτη χρεία 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.78.11}}; -εστάτη μάχη Anon.Hist.{{Refs|“Oxy.” 12ii31.}}
**II.3** as a title of divinities, τῶν -εστάτων θεῶν {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1179 (from Sparta)}}; also of Eastern Kings, e.g.Ptolemy V, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OG” 190.5(Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; Antiochus of Syria, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1a. 1, etc.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-νῶς** **openly, conspicuously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 5.105 (_superlative_).}}
**III.2** **with distinction,** λαμπρῶς καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.844 (from Phrygia), compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.3.1}}: _comparative_ -έστερον, ζην **with greater distinction,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 223.19.}}

2018:
** ἐπιφέρω**,
_future_ ἐποίσω: _aorist 1_ ἐπήνεγκα: _aorist 2_ ἐπήνεγκον: {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _aorist 1_ _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐποίση {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 654.21:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἐποισθήσεται· ἐπενεχθήσεται, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**bring, put** or **lay upon,** σοὶ..βαρείας χεῖρας ἐποίσει **will lay** heavy hands **upon** thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.89; χεῖρας ἐποίσει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.438; ἐπ᾽ ἀλλήλοισι φέρον πολύδακρυν Ἄρηα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.132, compare 8.516; ἐπ᾽ ἰχθύσι κῆρα φέρουσα 24.82; so ἐ. δόρυ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 766, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1192, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 344 (Lyric poetry)}}; and in Prose, ἐ. τινὶ πόλεμον **make** war **upon** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.81, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; ὅπλα [prev. author] 4.16, 7.18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.36}}; ἐ. τὸ διάφορόν τισι **to bring** discord **upon** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.55}}; ἐ. δίκην, τιμωρίαν τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 943d; ἀμοιβήν τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.84.10: _absolutely_, εἰ γὰρ ὧδ᾽ ἐποίσεις}} if you **shall pursue your attack** thus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 837.}}
**b** **inflict,** πληγήν, πληγάς τινι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.14.2, “PTeb.” 331.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; **vent,** ὀργήν {{NT|NT.Rom.3.5.}}
**2** **place upon,** especially of placing offerings on the grave, ἐ. ἀπαρχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58, compare 2.34; τῷ νεκρῷ στέφανον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 36; ταφὴν εἴς τινα 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.73}}; τὰ ἐπιφερόμενα the **offerings,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.1}}; **lay on, apply,** φάρμακον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 354b; ἐ. τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐπὶ τὰ πράγματα [prev. author] “Cra.” 424e.}}
**3** **bring** as a charge **against,** ἐ. τινὶ αἰτίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, compare 26, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98a; ἔγκλημα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 766 (troch.); μέμψιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1253 (Lyric poetry); ψόγον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; ἐ. μωρίην, μανίην τινί, **impute** it **to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131, 6.112; ἀδικίας -ομένης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42}}; τὴν Κλεοπάτραν αὐτῷ **cast** Cl.**in his teeth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.1; τι ἐπί τινα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143a27.}}
**4** **bring,** i.e. **confer, impose, upon,** in good or bad sense, ἐ. τιμὰν θνατοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.31; ἐλευθερίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85; δουλείαν [prev. author] 3.56.}}
**5** **add to, increase,** ἐ. τὴν ὑπερβολήν {{Refs|[prev. work] 82}}; ὀργὰς ἐπιφέρειν τινί **minister** to his passions, **gratify** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 230, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83 (cf. Scholia)}}; for ἐπὶ ἦρα φέρειν, see at {ἦρα}.
**6** **give** a name **to,** ὄνομα ἐ. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307b, “R.” 596a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408a7, +others}}; **assign** an attribute to a substantive, τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ χρώματα καὶ σχήματα καὶ κακίας καὶ ἀρετάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 251a.}}
**7** ἐ. ψῆφον **to give** a vote, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.14.}}
**8** **subjoin, add,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 60. 26, +others, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 34, +others, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 5.9.}}
**9** **adduce, cite,** Εὐριπίδεια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; παροιμίαν 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 122 (probably)}}; **produce** proofs, documents, etc., ἀποδείξεις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 257.19 (1st c.AD)}}; κυρία ἡ συγγραφή, ὅπου ἂν -φέρηται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 2.16 (3rd c.BC).}}
**10** in Logic, **assert as a conclusion** or **inference,** τὸ λῆγον, τὸ λοιπόν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.80; τὸ μὴ ἀκολουθοῦν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.21}}; ὅτι..{{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. work] 27; ὅσα ἐπιφέρουσιν ἄτοπα οἱ Ζήνωνος λόγοι 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.535S.:}} —_passive_, τὸ τοῖς ἐξ ὑποθέσεως ληφθεῖσιν ἐπιφερόμενον the conclusion which **follows from** the premises, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.89, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.7.16.}}
**II** ἐπιφέρων, ὁ, a throw at dice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 57.6.}}
**II** _middle_, **bring with** or **upon oneself, bring as a dowry,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.14, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.19}}; of soldiers, σιτία {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 13; ὕδωρ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.1.4.}}
**II.2** **consume (eat) in addition,** οἱ μὲν πίνουσι μόνον, οἱ δὲ σιτίον -ονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.3}}:—_passive_, -ομένη τροφή {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.52.}}
**II.3** **wear** or **carry on one's person,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.11.}}
**III** _passive_, **rush upon** or **after, attack, assault,** ὅς τις.. ἐπὶ νηυσὶ φέροιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.743}}; τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.19, etc.}}; of a ship, **bear down upon** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90}}; θάλαττα μεγάλη ἐπιφέρεται a great sea **strikes the ship,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20}}; **inveigh against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61}}: with _infinitive_, **to be eager to** do, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.24.5.}}
**III.b** of humours etc. in the body, τὸ -όμενον the **accumulation** of milk, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.77}}; διαφορεῖν τὸ ἐπενεχθέν disperse the **abscess,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.137; compare ἐπιφορά 1.5a.}}
**III.2** **to be borne onwards,** of a raft, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}; ἐ. ἐπί τι **to be led to** an opinion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 443a22 (assuming variant).}}
**III.3** **impend, threaten,** ἐ. κίνδυνος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.23.7}}; mostly in _participle_, προδεικνύειν τὰ ἐπιφερόμενα **coming events,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, compare 3.16; ἐ. κακά 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.7; ὑπεκστῆναι τὸν λόγον ἐπιφερόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 43a}}; τὰ ἐ. **the following** (in speaking or writing), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.6.8.}}
**III.4** of phrases, **to be applied,** ἡ λέξις -φέρεται τοῖς πράγμασιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.41c}}; but οἱ διὰ μακροῦ -φερόμενοι λόγοι sustained **outbursts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 196.}}
**III.5** Grammars, **follow,** of letters in a word, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 633.2, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.4, +others, etc.}}

2019:
** ἐπιφων-έω**,
**mention by name, tell of,** ἐπιφωνεῖν..ἱερὰν θήκην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1762 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.14}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|Ilias Parva “fragment” 2.}}
**b** **add a title,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.337.}}
**2** **say with respect to,** τινὶ ἐπιφώνημα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 3; εἴς τι [prev. author] “Luc.” 39; ἐπί τινος 1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 3}}; **apply** a phrase **to,** τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.178e}}: frequently of quoted sayings, ἐ. τὸ τραγικόν κτλ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.127, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 23, +others}}
**3** **call out, proclaim, exclaim,** ἐ. ὡς εὖ ἡμῖν βεβίωται {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 47; ἐπεφώνουν λέγοντες κτλ. NT.Luke.23.21, compare “PRyl.” 77.33 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐ. τινὶ καλῶς λέγειν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|Aristeas 196, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.17,+others, “lG” 14.830 (Puteoli, 2nd c.AD)}}; τῷ παρὰ δεῖπνον ἀκράτῳ προσδιδομένῳ τὸν Ἀγαθὸν ἐπιφωνοῦσι Δαίμονα Philonid._middle_ cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.675b:}} —_passive_, τὸ τοῖς γαμοῦσιν -φωνούμενον ταλασίῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 4.}}
**4** **respond,** in ritual, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.1.23.}}
**5** **subjoin, add as a finishing touch,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 107}}:—_passive_,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 110, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.9.}}

2020:
** ἐπιφώσκω**,
=ἐπιφαύσκω, **grow towards daylight, dawn,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.28.1, NT.Luke.23.54, “PLond.” 1.130.39 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** _transitive_, **let shine forth,** φέγγος poetical **de herb.**{{Refs|24.}}

2021:
** ἐπιχειρ-έω**,
(χείρ) **put one's hand to,** οἱ μὲν δείπνῳ ἐπεχείρεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.386, compare 395; πηδαλίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 542.}}
**2** **put one's hand to** a work, **set to work at, attempt,** τῇ διώρυχι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.158}}; δρησμῷ ἐ. **attempt** an escape, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.70; τῇ ὁδῷ [prev. author] 7.43, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 819; τοῖσι βασιληΐοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.61; τυραννίδι [prev. author] 5.46; ἔργῳ τοσούτῳ [prev. author] 9.27}}; λόγοις, τέχνῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a, “Grg.” 521d, etc.; τοῖς ἀδυνάτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.41, etc.}}
**3** less frequently with _accusative_, μεγάλα ἔργα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 75; δίκαιον πρᾶγμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45c, “Phlb.” 57b}}:—_passive_, **to be attempted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, 6.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.41, etc.; τὸ ἐπιχειρούμενον}}**the thing attempted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746b.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **endeavour, attempt to** do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38, 65, 9.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 81, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40, etc.}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.7.3}}:—_passive_, ἃ τὸ πρῶτον ἐπεχειρήθη πραχθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 337d, compare [prev. author] “Ti.” 53a, +others}}
**5** ἐπειχειρήθη with _dative_, **an operation was performed,** τῇσιν αἱμορροΐσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.20.}}
**II** **make an attempt on, attack,** τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, 26, 190, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.94, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1030, etc.; πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21; ἐπί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241d (but ἐπὶ τὴν τοῦ σώματος διαφθοράν with a view to.., 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a24); εἰς τὰς σατραπείας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.80: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72, 8.108, etc.}}; κτείνων ἢ ἐπιχειρῶν Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.98}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11.}}
**II.b** obscene meaning, ἐ. μειρακίοις {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 359d.}}
**III** **attempt to prove, argue dialectically,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 205a, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 3.4; περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 101a30}}; ἔκ τινος from a topic,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 115a26; ἔς τι 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.28; ἐ. ὅτι.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 128b26: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “APr.” 66a34; λογικώτερον ἔστιν ἐπιχειρεῖν ὧδε [prev. author] “Cael.” 275b12.}}

2022:
** ἐπιχέω**,
_future_ -χέω, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἐπιχεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 169:}}_aorist 1_ ἐπέχε; _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ I ἐπέχευα, _infinitive_ ἐπιχεῦαι (see. below):—
**A** **pour over,** χέρνιβα δ᾽ ἀμφίπολος προχόῳ ἐπέχευε..νίψασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.136, etc.; in full, χερσὶν ὕδωρ ἐπιχεῦαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.303; χερσὶ δ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ὕδωρ χευάντων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.213, etc.; also οἴνῳ ἐπιχεῖν ὕδωρ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.9.}}
**A.2** _metaphorically_, τοῖσι δ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ὕπνον ἔχευε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.445; Τρῶες δ᾽ ἐπὶ δούρατ᾽ ἔχευαν 5.618; ἀνέμων ἐπ᾽ ἀϋτμένα χεῦε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.289}}; θρῆνον **pour** a lament **over** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).64 (tmesis); ὀδμήν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.191 (tmesis)}}; βλασφημιῶν ἐ. (_genitive_ _partitive_) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 35.}}
**A.3** of solids, **heap up,** θανόντι χυτὴν ἐπὶ γαῖαν ἔχευαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.258, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.256; ἐπὶ σῆμ᾽ ἔχεεν 6.419}}:—_middle_, ὕπερθ᾽ ἐπὶ σῆμα χέεσθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.205.}}
**A.II** **pour in,** ἀπαντλοῦντα καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407d; ἓν ἀγαθὸν ἐπιχέασα, τρί᾽ ἐπαντλεῖ κακά 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 107 codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes}}; **fill** a cup, Ναννοῦς καὶ Λύδης ἐπίχει δύο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.168 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus).}}
**B** _middle_, **pour** or **throw over oneself,** χύσιν δ᾽ ἐπεχεύατο φύλλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.487; κατακλιθεὶς ἐπιχείσθω τὴν πέριξ ἄμμον 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.8.4}}; ἐπεχεύατο πήχεε παιδί she **threw her** arms **round** the boy, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.268; but πολλὴν ἐπεχεύατο ὕλην}}**for himself,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.257.}}
**B.2** **pour itself over,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.604.}}
**B.3** **anoint oneself,** ἀπὸ δείπνου {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 4.22.}}
**B.II** **have poured out for one to drink,** ἐ. ἄκρατόν τινος **drink** it to any one's health or honour, especially of lovers' toasts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.18, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 81.2 codices 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus}}; ἔρωτος ἀκράτω (_genitive_ _partitive_) ἐπεχεῖτο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.152; also simply ἐπιχεῖσθαί τινος 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 31J.}}
**C** _passive_, **to be poured over,** ἰλύος ἐπιχυθείσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.12: _metaphorically_, τοῖς Ἑλληνικοῖς ὀνόμασι τῶν Ἰταλικῶν ἐπικεχυμένων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 15.}}
**C.2** _metaphorically_, of a crowd, **stream on** or **in pursuit,** ἐπέχυντο (_Epic dialect_ _aorist 2_ _passive_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.654; ἀνὰ νῆας 16.295}}; so, **come like a stream over,** τοῖσι ἐναντίοισι ἐπιχυθέντας..μῦς ἀρουραίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141; τοσούτων μοι πραγμάτων ἐπικεχυμένων 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217c.}}
**C.3** **to be poured in as an addition,** τοῦ νῦν ἐπικεχυμένου λόγου, of the discussion, **that has** now **been started,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302c; ὁ νυνδὴ λόγος ἡμῖν ἐπιχυθείς [prev. author] “Lg.” 793b.}}
**C.II** **to be drowned in,** ἰχθῦς νάπυϊ ἐπικεχυμένους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 47.}}

2025:
** ἐπι-χρίω** [ῑ],
**anoint, besmear,** ἐπιχρίοντες ἀλοιφῇ (i.e. τὸ τόξον) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.179; χρῶτ᾽ ἀπονιψαμένη καὶ ἐπιχρίσασα παρειάς 18.172:}} —_middle_, χρῶτ᾽ ἀπονίπτεσθαι καὶ ἐπιχρίεσθαι ἀλοιφῇ{{Refs|[prev. work] 179.}}
**2** **plaster over,** τινι with a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 62.}}
**II** **lay on** ointment, μετὰ τὸ -χρισθῆναι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Zopyrus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 14.58.1}}; κροτάφοις -χριόμενα variant in {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.22; πηλὸν ἐπὶ τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς NT.John.9.6, compare “IG” 14.966 (Rome, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **use for anointing,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.270.}}

2026:
** ἐποικοδομ-έω**,
**build up,** -ήσαντας αὐτὸ (i.e. τὸ τεῖχος) ὑψηλότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.25}}: _metaphorically_, **pile up, use a climax,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1365a16, “Rh.Al.” 1426b3.}}
**2** **build upon,** ἐπὶ κρηπῖδι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.11; ἐπὶ κρηπῖδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736e; ἐπὶ τοὺς τοίχους “OGI” 483.117 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 793c (_passive_)}}; φύσει μαθήματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.610}}; τινὶ εὐτονίαν, ἀσφάλειαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.15.8 (_passive_)}}; ἐπὶ θεμέλιον or θεμελίῳ, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Cor.3.12, NT.Eph.2.20; θεμελίοις 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.47 (_passive_)}}; τοῖς ἀληθέσιν ἐψευσμένα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.2.6, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 87 (_passive_).}}
**2.b** **edify,** ἑαυτοὺς τῇ πίστει {{NT|NT.Jude.20}}:—_passive_, -ούμενοι ἐν Χριστῷ {{NT|NT.Col.2.7.}}
**II** ={ἐπιτειχίζω}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.46.5}}:—_middle_, στρατόπεδα πέντε -ησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.2.}}

2027:
** ἐποκέλλω**,
={ἐπικέλλω}, **run ashore,** νέας, τὴν νέα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.16, 7.182; πλοῖα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26.}}
**2** of the ship, **run aground, be wrecked,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.102, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.15}}; **put in,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.23.3}}; of tunnies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 844a30.}}

2028:
** ἐπονομ-άζω**,
**apply** a word (_accusative_) **as a name** to a thing (_dative_), ᾧ γένει κέραμον ἐπωνομάκαμεν to which sort we **have given the name** pottery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60d; ᾧ τὸ ἔστιν ἐπονομάζεις [prev. author] “Tht.” 185c; πᾶσι ταὐτὸν ἐ. ὄνομα [prev. author] “Plt.” 263c (reversely, τίς Ἀλεξάνδρῳ τὸν ἑαυτοῦ βίον ἐπονομάζει καθάπερ Πλάτωνι}}; who **dedicates** his life to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}, **calls** himself an Alexandrist? {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 31.354b):}} —_passive_, τῇ ἀρχῇ ὕβρις ἐπωνομάσθη **the name** insolence **was given to** this rule, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a, compare “Cra.” 404b.}}
**2** **call by** a name, ἀπὸ τοῦ θεῖν θεοὺς αὐτοὺς ἐπονομάσαι [φαίνονται] {{Refs|[prev. work] 397d}}; τὰς Μούσας ἀπὸ τοῦ μῶσθαι τὸ ὄνομα τοῦτο ἐ. **called** the Muses **by** this name (viz. Muses),{{Refs|[prev. work] 406a; ἐ. αὐτὰ τῇ ἐκείνων ἐπωνυμίᾳ [prev. author] “Phd.” 103b}}; with εἶναι _pleonastic_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 133d}}:—_passive_, **to be named,** ἀπό τινος after one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2, etc.}}; also τινος, **to be named** the temple **of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1329, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 738b (but in 626d the _genitive_ depends on ἄξιος)}}; πατρόθεν ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ly.” 204e}}; πατρὸς.. δαῖτ᾽ ἐπωνομασμένην, i.e. **called after** Agamemnon (compare ἐπώνυμος), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 284}}; especially **to be surnamed,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; Ἰουδαῖος ἐπονομάζῃ NT.Rom.2.17.}}
**3** generally, **name, call** so and so, ἀφνειὸν ἐ. τὸ χωρίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13; σοφιστὴν ἐ. σεαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 349a, compare “Phd.” 113b, +others; παρακαταθήκην ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.15.}}
**4** **pronounce** a name, ἐ. τὰ οὐνόματα ἐν τῷ ὕμνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.35, compare 7.117}}; ἐπονομάζων τινά **uttering** his **name** as he throws the cottabus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 273, compare 4th c.BC: Clearchus Comicus 1.}}

2029:
** ἐποπτ-εύω**,
(ἐπόπτης) **overlook, watch,** of an overseer, ἔργα τ᾽ ἐποπτεύεσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.140, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 767; Ἑρμῆ..πατρῶ᾽ ἐποπτεύων κράτη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1; ἐ. μάχην [prev. work] 489}}; ὁ πάντ᾽ ἐ. τάδε ἥλιος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 993(985); δίκας [prev. author] “Eu.” 224; ἄλλοτε δ᾽ ἄλλον ἐποπτεύει Χάρις φόρμιγγι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.11; αἰῶνας -εύουσα χελιδών “IGRom.” 4.235 (hexameters).}}
**2** **visit, punish,** κότῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 220.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **keep watch,** οἱ περὶ τοὺς νόμους ἐποπτεύοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951d.}}
**II** **become an** ἐπόπτης, **be admitted to the highest gkade at the mysteries,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 333e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 26}}: with _accusative_, **view as an** ἐπόπτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250c}}: proverbial, of **attaining to the highest earthly happiness,** ἐποπτεύειν δοκῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 745.}}
**III** **study, meditate on,** with _dative_, καθαρῇσιν ἐ. μελέτῃσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 110.2. (Cf. ἐπιοπτεύω.)}}

2030:
** ἐπόπτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(ἐπόψομαι) **overseer, watcher,** especially of a god, Πυθῶνος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.5, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 266; ὁ πάντων ἐ. θεός LXX.Est.5.1}}; title of Poseidon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.30.1}}; of the Sun, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 666.25 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; δαίμονες ἐ. τῶν ἀνθρωπίνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 105a; ὥσπερ ἐπόπτας τῶν στρατηγουμένων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.25}}; ἐ. γῆς καὶ θαλάσσης, of Pompey, {{Refs|“JHS” 27.64 (from Cyzicus)}}; of Augustus, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.309 (Pergam.)}}; ἐ. εἰρήνης, of a police **magistrate,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 991 (4th c.AD).}}
**2** simply, **spectator,** πόνων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 301.}}
**3** **inspector,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 10.16.13 “Intr.”}}
**II** **one admitted to the highest grade of the mysteries,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.6.51, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 22, etc., compare ἐφόπται “IG” 12(8).205.3 (from Samothrace): with _genitive_, μυστηρίων ἐ. “Michel” 1141 ([prev. passage])}}; τινος {{NT|“PMag.Lond.” 121.572: _metaphorically_, ἐ. τῆς ἐκείνου μεγαλειότητος NT.2Pet.1.16.}}

2031:
** ἔπος**,
older **ϝέπος** {{Refs|“SIG” 9 (see. below)}}, etc., εος, τό (Sanskrit _vácas_ 'word', 'hymn', compare εἶπον):
**I** **word,** παύρῳ ἔπει in short **utterance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.98; ἐπέων κόσμος 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.52, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 21; ἔπους σμικροῦ χάριν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 443; λόγοι ἔπεσι κοσμηθέντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67}}: generally, **that which is uttered in words, speech, tale,** ἔπος ἐρέειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.83, etc.; φάσθαι 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 7.3, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.23, etc.}}; joined with μῦθος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.597, 11.561.}} —Special uses,
**I.1** **song** or **lay accompanied by music,** {{Refs|8.91, 17.519.}}
**I.2** **pledged word, promise,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.8}}; τελέσαι ἔπος fulfil, keep **one's word,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|14.44, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1033.}}
**I.3** **word in season, counsel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.216, 2.807, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.166, etc.; frequently in Trag., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 513, etc.}}
**I.4** **word of a deity, oracle,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.266, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13, etc.}}
**I.5** **saying, proverb,** τὸ παλαιὸν ἔπος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.51, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 507.}}
**I.6** **word,** opposed to **deed,** ἔπε᾽ ἀκράαντα **words** of none effect, opposed to ἔτυμα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.565, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 111 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to ἔργον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.234, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.272, etc., compare 11.1; αἴτε ϝέπος αἴτε ϝάργον “SIG” 9 (Elis, 6th c.BC)}}; opposed to βίη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.106; opposed to χεῖρες, 1.77 (_plural_).}}
**I.7** **subject of a speech, message,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.652, 17.701, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1144, etc.}}
**II** later usages,
**II.1** joined with ἔργον or πρᾶγμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 174 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 39, etc.; ἔργῳ τε καὶ ἔπει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879c; ἅμα ἔπος τε καὶ ἔργον ἐποίεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134; χρηστὰ ἔργα καὶ ἔπεα ποιέειν [prev. author] 1.90.}}
**II.2** κατ᾽ ἔπος **word by word,** κατ᾽ ἔ. βασανιεῖν φησι τὰς τραγῳδίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 802.}}
**II.3** πρὸς ἔπος at **the first word,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ep.Sat.” 37.}}
**II.3.b** **word** in exchange for **word,** ἀμείβεσθαι, ἀποκρίνεσθαι, of an oracle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Alex.” 19, “Philops.” 38; also ἔ. δ᾽ ἀμείβου πρὸς ἔ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 586, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1375, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217d.}}
**II.3.c** οὐδὲν πρὸς ἔ. to no **purpose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 751}}; also, nothing to **the purpose,** ἐὰν μηδὲν πρὸς ἔ. ἀποκρίνωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 295c, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 36}}; τί πρὸς ἔπο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 18d.}}
**II.4** ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν **almost, practically,** qualifying a too absolute expression, especially with πᾶς and οὐδείς (not with metaphors), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17a, “Phd.” 78e, “Grg.” 456a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009b16, “Pol.” 1252b29, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.47, etc.}}; opposed to ὄντως or ἀκριβεῖ λόγῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 656e, “R.” 341b; later ὡς ἔ. ἐστὶν εἰπεῖν “POxy.” 67.14 (4th c.AD)}}; in Trag., ὡς εἰπεῖν ἔ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 714 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 167, “Hipp.” 1162, once in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Lg.” 967b (assuming variant).}}
**II.5** ἑνὶ ἔπει in one **word, briefly,** ἑνὶ ἔπεϊ πάντα συλλαβόντα λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82.}}
**III** of single **words,** especially with _reference_ to etymology or usage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 638, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339a, etc.}}; ὀρθότης ἐπῶν,=ὀρθοέπεια (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1181; ἄριστ᾽ ἐπῶν ἔχον [prev. work] 1161.}}
**IV** in _plural_, **epic poetry,** opposed to μέλη (lyric poetry), ἰαμβεῖα, διθύραμβοι, etc., ῥαπτῶν ἐπέων ἀοιδοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.2; τὰ Κύπρια ἔπεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.117, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379a, etc.; ἔπεά τε ποιεῖν πρὸς λύραν τ᾽ ἀείδειν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 21.6; νικήσας ἔπος “IG” 3.1020; ποητὴς ἐπῶν [prev. work]7.3197.9 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_),compare “OGI” 51.37 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC).}}
**IV.b** generally, **poetry,** even lyrics, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 25 (probably), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.8, etc.}}
**IV.c** **lines, verses,** especially of spoken **lines** in the drama, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 862, 956, etc.}}: _singular_, **verse, line** of poetry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 319d}}; **group of verses,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 386c, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.143.}}
**IV.d** **lines** of writing, μυρίων ἐπῶν μῆκος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.136}}; ἐν ὅλοις ἑπτὰ ἔπεσι παραδραμεῖν, of a historian, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 28.}}

2032:
** ἐπουράνιος** [ᾰ], ον,
_Epic dialect_ η, ον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus (see.below), 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 2.429}}:— **heavenly,** in {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only of the gods, ἐ. θεός, θεοί, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.484, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.129, +others; εὐσεβέων ἐ. ψυχαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 132.3; πατήρ NT.Matt.18.35}}; ἡ ἐ. πορεία _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256d.}}
**2** _plural_, as _substantive_, οἱ ἐ., ={θεοί}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.5, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.21}}; opposed to ἐπίγειοι, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Phil.2.11; so ἤδη ἐ. εἶ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 4.3}}; τὰ ἐ., ={τὰ μετέωρα}, variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19b (ἐ. σώματα NT.1Cor.15.40).}}
**3** **up to heaven,** ἔπτατ᾽ ἐπουρανίη variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 134.}}

2033:
** ἑπτά**, οἱ, αἱ, τά,
_indeclinable_ **seven,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.421, etc.}}; as a mystical number, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1093a13, etc.}}; αἱ ἑ. νῆσοι the **seven** largest islands, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 268, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 837a31}}; τὰ ἑ. θεάματα the {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Seven” Wonders, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.33, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.11, etc.}}; οἱ ἑ. σοφισταί the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Seven” Sages, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.109, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.311 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; οἱ ἑ. σοφοί 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.172}}; οἱ ἑ. alone, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.40, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 286.3.}}
**2** οἱ ἑ., board of magistrates at Olbia, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 495.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; οἱ ἑ. ἄνδρες, = Latin _septemviri epulones,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.32. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sept[macutenull],** cf. Sanskrit _saptá,_ Latin _septem_ (fancifully connected with σέβομαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.30, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 43): 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus has τεπτά, i.e. ηεπτά.)}}

2036:
** εἶπον**
(_present_ ἔπω is used by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 429, 490, etc., but the}}_present_ in use is φημί, λέγω, ἀγορεύω (see. infr. IV), the _future_ ἐρέω, ἐρῶ, the _perfect_ εἴρηκα), _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry ἔειπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.552, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.25}}; _subjunctive_ εἴπω (_Epic dialect_ εἴπωμι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.392, -ῃσθα 11.224, -ῃσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.87)}}; _optative_ εἴποιμ; _infinitive_ εἰπεῖν, _Epic dialect_ -έμεναι, -έμεν, {{Refs|7.375, 9.688, _Doric dialect_ εἴπην (see. below)}}; _participle_ εἰπών: also _aorist 1_ εἶπα (ἔειπα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.153), ὅπερ εἶπα}} as I **said**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus “Vit.Eur.fragment” 39 xvii 14}} mostly in _Ionic dialect_ Prose, also {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.26, “UPZ” 62.14 (2nd c.BC)}}, and the _2nd pers._ _indicative_ and _imperative_ of this form are preferred in _Attic dialect_, _2nd pers._ _singular_ _indicative_ εἶπας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.106, 108, etc.}}; _imperative_ εἶπον (on the accent see{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hdn.Gr. 1.460) 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 154, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 891, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.11, εἰπάτω (ἀν-ειπάτω “IG” 22.1186.19 (4th c.BC), but ἀν-ειπέτω [prev. work]1247.13 (3rd c.BC))}}, -ατον, -ατ; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶπαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 333.3 (Samos, 4th c.BC), later εἴπασαν “IG” 7.2225.51 (from Thisbe)}}; _participle_ εἴπας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 42}}, _Aeolic dialect_ εἴπαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.46, compare 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 156}}; in compounds _middle_ ἀπείπασθαι (which see), διείπασθαι (which see), but never in good _Attic dialect_: (_reduplicate_ _aorist 2_ from ϝεπ- 'say'; ϝείπην only conjecture in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2; ϝεῖπαι “Leg.Gort.” 8.15}}; with ἔ- (ϝ) ειπον cf. Sanskrit _avocam_, _reduplicate_ _aorist_ of **vac-** 'say'; compare ἔπος):—**speak, say**, ὣς εἰπών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.68, etc.; τινί 17.692, etc.; εἰς ἅπαντας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 303}}; εἰπεῖν ἔν τισιν or μετά τισιν **speak** among a number, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.445, 3.85, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἔπος, μῦθον, θεοπρόπιον, οὐνόματα, etc., 3.204, 1.552, 85, 17.260, etc.}}; τινί τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.169, +others}}; τι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica}} [same places], etc.; τι ἔς or πρός τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 487, “Aj.” 292}}; εἰπεῖν περί τινος, ἀμφί τινι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.347, 14.364: with _genitive_, πατρός τε καὶ υἱέος}} **of** them, {{Refs|11.174}}; εἰπεῖν ὅτι or ὡς **to say** that.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.655, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.373, etc.}}: but also with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473a, etc.}} b. **recite**, ἔπη {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 535b.}}
**2** in parenthesis, ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν so **to say**, limiting a general statement, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 714, etc.}}; **speaking loosely**, opposed to ὄντως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 656e}}; opposed to ἀκριβεῖ λόγῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 341b; ὡς εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258e, +others; ὡς ἀξίως εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 651b36: without ὡς, οὐ πολλῷ λόγῳ εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61; ἐς τὸ ἀκριβὲς εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82; σχεδὸν εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 237c: καθόλου εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 12a27; ἡ ἁπλῶς εἰπεῖν ἀπόδειξις [prev. author] “APo.” 75b23}}; τὸ ξύμπαν εἶπαι, εἰπεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.143, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138.}}
**3** εἴποι τις **as** one **might say**, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.35.1; ὥσπερ εἴποι τις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 180 (assuming variant); ὡς εἴποι τις 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 64.5 (assuming variant).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **address, accost** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.210, etc.}}
**II.2** **name, mention**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.90, etc.}}
**II.3** **call** one so and so, πολλοί τέ μιν ἐσθλὸν ἔειπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.334, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 43, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 465, etc.}}
**II.4** with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, **tell** or **proclaim** so **of** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.479 (where ἀνιόντα depends on εἴποι)}}; εἰπεῖν τινα ὅτι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.22; ἀτάσθαλόν τι εἰ. τινά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.314; κακὰ εἰ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 649; μηδὲν φλαῦρον εἰ. τ. [prev. author] “Nu.” 834; ἐῢ εἰ. τινά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.302}}; εἰ. τεθνεῶτ᾽ Ὀρέστην **speak of** him **as** dead, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 682.}}
**II.5** **celebrate**, of poets, Αἴαντος βίαν {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.2.6 (Antip. Sid.).}}
**III** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **order** or **command** one to.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.76, 22.262, etc.; also εἰπεῖν πρός τινα, with}}_infinitive_, {{Refs|16.151}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, εἶπον τὰς παῖδας δεῦρ᾽ ἄγειν τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 932, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59e, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.26: followed by ἵνα}}, frequently in **NT,** {{NT|NT.Matt.4.3, +others}}
**IV** **propose, move** a measure in the assembly, εἰπὼν τὰ βέλτιστα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.12; εἰπεῖν τὰ δέοντα [prev. work]15; εἶπε ψήφισμα [prev. author] 24.11}}: frequently as a formal prefix to decrees and laws, Λάχης εἶπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, compare “IG” 12.24, +others}}; compare ἀγορεύω.
**V** **plead**, δίκην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.508; δικίδιον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 347.}}
**VI** **promise, offer**, χρυσὸν εἶφ᾽ ὃς ἂν κτάνῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 33.}}
**VII** _imperative_ εἰπέ sometimes used in addressing several persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 328, “Av.” 366, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.10.}}
** ἔπω**,
**A** **say, call, name,** ἤν Πέρσειον ἔπουσιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 429, compare 490, “Th.” 508. (Prob. invented by 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus, as _present_ of εἶπον.)}}

2038:
** ἐργάζομαι**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.469}}, etc., Cretan dialect **ϝεργάδδομαι** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 181v5}}: _future_ **-άσομαι** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1116}}, etc., _Doric dialect_ {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|ἐργαξοῦμαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.23, ἐργῶμαι “PCair.Zen.” 107.4 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Gen.29.27, +others, “IG” 7.3073.12 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC) (but 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus ἐργᾷ· ἐργάζει)}}: _aorist_ εἰργασάμην, _Ionic dialect_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐργ- 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Th.” 845 (Lyric poetry), etc., 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _plural_ _optative_ ἐργασαίατο Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 1147, “Lys.” 42; _Doric dialect_ ἠργάξαντο “SIG” 248 M (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}]: _perfect_ εἴργασμαι, _Ionic dialect_{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἔργ- 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.έ, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “fragment” 311, etc.}} —These tenses are used both in _middle_ and _passive_ _significations_: for other _passive_ tenses, see below{{Refs 4th c.BC+|111:— _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. of 4th C have ἠργαζόμην, ἠργασάμην, (ἐξ-) “IG” 22.1585.11, 1669.10, +others, but εἴργασμαι [prev. work]1666 A 27; so also ἠργάσατο [prev. work]7.424 (Oropus, 4th c.BC), εἰργασμένος [prev. work]3073.51 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC), ἐξήργασατο “UPZ” 19.8 (2nd c.BC), εἴργασμαι “PCair.Zen.” 146.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; but this rule is often broken in later Papyrus, Inscrr., and codices:—**work, labour,** especially of husbandry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 299, 309, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.72, etc.; but also of all manual labour, of slaves, ἐ. ἀνάγκῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.272}}; of quarrymen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.124, etc.; τὴν οὐσίαν οὐ δικαζόμενον ἀλλ᾽ ἐργαζόμενον κεκτημένον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12}}; ἐ. ἐν τοῖς ἔργοις in the mines, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.31: with _dative_ _instrumental_, χαλκῷ with brass, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 151; also of animals, βοῦς ἐργάτης ἐργάζεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 563}}; of birds **working** to get food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 616b35}}; of bees, {{Refs|[prev. work] 625b22}}; of Hephaestus' self-acting bellows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.469; τὸ χρῆμ᾽ ἐργάζεται}} the matter **works,** i.e. **goes on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ec.” 148}}; ὁ ἀὴρ ἐργάζεται **produces an effect** [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “de Causis Plantarum” 5.12.7}}]; οὐχ ὁμοίως ἐργάσεται τὸ θερμόν {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.18.11.}}
**II** _transitive_, **work at, make,** ἔργα κλυτά, of Athena, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.72, compare 22.422; ἀγάλματα, ὕμνους, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.1, “I.” 2.46; τρίποδα, Νίκην, “SIG” 34 (Delph., 5th c.BC); ἁμαξίδας Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Nubes” 880; οἰκοδόμημα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76}}]; εἰκόνας, ἀνδριάντας, καλὰ ἔργα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cra.” 431c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 2.6.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Men.” 91d; κηρόν, σχαδόνας, of bees, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 627a6, 30; μέλι 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit. Hippocr.” 11}}; **make** so and so, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ξηρὸν ἐ. τινά 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 11.2; μέγαν Aelianus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Varia Historia” 3.1}}].
**II.2** **do, perform,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔργα ἀεικέα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.733}}; ἔργον ἐπ᾽ ἔργῳ ἐ., of husbandmen, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 382, compare 397; ἐργασίας ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121b33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oec.” 7.20; ἐναίσιμα, φίλα ἐ., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.321, 24.210; θαυμαστά Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 213d; περὶ θεοὺς ἄδικον μηδέν [prev\. author] “Grg.” 522d}}]; ἐ. πρᾶγμα, opposed to βουλεύειν, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ant.” 267, compare “OT” 347; τὸ ἔργον Κυρίου NT.1Cor.16.10}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **do** something **to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τά περ νῦν ἐ. [ὁ ἥλιος] τὸν Νεῖλον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26}}, etc.; chiefly in bad sense, **do** one ill, **do** one a shrewd turn, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κακὰ ἐργάζεσθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 786, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, etc.; so οἷά μ᾽ εἰργάσω, τί μ᾽ ἐργάσε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 928, 1172 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μὴ δῆτα τοῦτό μ᾽ ἐργάσῃ [prev\. author] “El.” 1206; αἴσχιστα ἐ. τινά Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 787}}]; less frequently, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀγαθὰ ἐ. τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.79, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cri.” 53a; πολλὰ καὶ καλὰ τὴν Ἑλλάδα [prev\. author] “Phdr.” 244b}}; seldom [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τινί τι Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 1350; οἷν ἐμοὶ δυοῖν ἔργ᾽ ἐστὶ κρείσσον᾽ ἀγχόνης εἰργασμένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1374}}].
**II.2.b** **perform** rites, {{Refs|τὰ ἱερὰ ἐ. “1st Epistle to the Corinthians” 9.13}}.
**II.2.c** in Law, ζημίαν ἐ. **do** damage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.20, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Ath.” 22.}}
**II.3** **work** a material, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ὅπλα..οἷσίν τε χρυσὸν ἐργάζετο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.435}}; ἐ. γῆν **till** the land, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, etc.; ἐ. [ἀγροὺς] ἐργάταις Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.11; γῆν καὶ ξύλα καὶ λίθους [prev\. author] “HG” 3.3.7}}]; [ἀργυρῖτιν] Docum. cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.28; ἐ. θάλασσαν, of traders, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.46; γλαυκὴν ἐ., of fishers, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” 440.}}
**II.4** **earn by working,** [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|χρήματα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 840, etc.; καινὸν βίον ἐκ τοῦ δικαίου Andocides Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 1.144, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 43; ἀργύριον ἀπὸ σοφίας Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Hp.Ma.” 282d; μισθοῦ τὰ ἐπιτήδεια Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 2.8.2}}].
**II.5** **work at, practise,** μουσικήν, τέχνας, etc., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 60e, “R.” 374a, etc.; ἐπιστήμας Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Oeconomicus” 1.7}}]; ἀρετὴν καὶ σωφροσύνην variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.6; δικαιοσύνην, ἀνομίαν, NT.Acts.10.35, NT.Matt.7.23.}}
**II.6** _absolutely_, **work at a trade** or **business, traffic, trade,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐν [γναφείῳ] 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.2; ἐν ἐμπορίῳ καὶ χρήμασιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.44; ἐν τῇ ἀγορᾷ [prev\. author] 57.31}} (also οἱ τὴν τετράγωνον (i.e. ἀγοράν) ἐργαζόμενοι those who **trade in** the square, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“BCH” 8.126 (compare “Glotta” 14.73)); κατὰ θάλατταν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.48}}; τούτοις..ναυτικοῖς ἐ. **trade** with this money on bottomry, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 33.4; δὶς ἢ τρὶς ἐ. τῷ αὐτῷ ἀργυρίῳ [prev\. author] 56.30}}; ταῦτα ἐ. thus he **trades,** {{Refs|[prev\. author] 25.82; οἱ ἐργαζόμενοι _traders,_ [prev\. author] 34.51}}; οἱ ἐν Δήλῳ ἐ., = Latin _qui Deli negotiantur_, {{Refs|“CIG” 2285b; especially of courtesans, σώματι ἐ.}} Latin _quaestum corpore facere,_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.20; ἐπὶ τέγους ἀπὸ τοῦ σώματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.13.2}}; ἀπὸ τῆς ὥρας Alexis Sam. cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.572f, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tim.” 14.}}
**II.7** **cause,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κολακείην 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 268; πημονάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 326; πόθον τινί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.11; σύριγγας ἀνιάτους 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.44}}.
**III** _passive_, rarely in _present_ and _imperfect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.87 (ἐξ-), 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Eux.” 35}}: _future_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐργασθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1218, (ἐξ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Ep.” 6.8}}: _perfect_ εἴργασμαι (see. below): _aorist 1_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|εἰργάσθην Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 353a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 6.3.2, etc.}}]
**III.1** **to be made** or **built,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἔργαστο τὸ τεῖχος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179; ἐκ πέτρας εἰργασμένος Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 244; οἰκοδόμημα διὰ ταχέων εἰργ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}]; λίθοι εἰργ. **wrought** stones, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 1.93; γῆ εἰργ. Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Oeconomicus” 19.8; θώρακας εὖ εἰργ. [prev\. author] “Mem.” 3.10.9}}].
**III.2** **to be done,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 354, 1346, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hec.” 1085; εἰργασμένα _things done, deeds, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.53 (ἐργ-), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1281, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 1369.}}

2039:
** ἐργᾰσ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, Cretan dialect **ϝεργασία** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 8.44, ἡ, (ἐργάτης)}} **work, business,** {{Refs|ἐργασίην φεύγουσα “hymnus ad Mercurium” 486}}, etc.; opposed to ἀργία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 2.7.7; ἐ. ἀγαθή}} productive **labour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Vect.” 4.29; ἐργασίᾳ ἐγχειρεῖν, of bees, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 625b24; ἡ περὶ τὴν θάλατταν ἐ., of seamen, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 371b; μὴ γενομένης ἐργασίας}} if no **work** was done, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.20; δὸς ἐργασίαν}} with _infinitive_, Latin _da operam ut..,_ NT.Luke.12.58, compare [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“OGI” 441.109 (_SC. de Stratonicensibus,_ 1st c.BC): _plural_, τὰς ἐν ὑπαίθρῳ ἐ. ἐργάζεσθαι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Oeconomicus” 7.20; ἐ. ἀνελεύθεροι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121b33, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “fragment” 196 (uncertain).}}]
**2** **function,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἥπατος 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.15}}.
**II** **working at, making, manufacture,** ἱματίων, ὑποδημάτων, etc., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Grg.” 449d, “Tht.” 146d, etc.; ἡ τῆς ἐσθῆτος ἐκ τῶν ἐρίων ἐ. Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Oeconomicus” 7.21}}]; **making up** of a prescription, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Ulc.” 14: _metaphorically_, Πέργαμος ἀμφὶ τεαῖς χερὸς ἐργασίαις ἁλίσκεται Troy is (i.e. is doomed to be)}} taken in the part **wrought by** thy **hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.42; ἐ. ἡδονῆς}} **production** of pleasure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 353d; ἐ. χρημάτων}} money-**making,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1160a16 (but _administration_ of property, “Leg.Gort.” [same place]).}}
**II.2** **working** of a material, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἡ ἐ. τοῦ σιδήρου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68}}; χαλκοῦ, ἐρίων, ξύλων, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Chrm.” 173e; τῶν χρυσείων μετάλλων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Eux.” 36; πίττης Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Historia Plantarum” 9.2.6}}]: most commonly, **tillage** of the ground, ἐ. γῆς, χώρας, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” 1034 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.30, etc.; ἐ. κήπων Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Minos” 316b}}]; ἐ. περὶ τὴν τροφήν **preparation** (i.e. mastication and digestion) of food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Juv.” 469a3}}; **treatment** of silphium, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 6.3.2}}
**II.3** generally, **trade, business,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 3.10.1; ἐπὶ τῆς ἐργασίας ὢν τῆς κατὰ θάλατταν}} engaged in **trade** by sea, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.4; ἡ ἐ. τῆς τραπέζης}} the banking **business,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev\. author] 36.6; ἐ. χρυσοχοϊκή, ἀρωματική, “PLond.” 3.906.6 (2nd c.AD), “PFay.” 93.7 (2nd c.AD); βαφεῖς τὴν ἐ.}} dyers by **trade,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 287.3(2nd c.AD)}}; especially of **a courtesan's trade,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.135, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129}}; of sexual **intercourse,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr.” 876a39.}}
**II.3.b** ἐὰν ἐργασίαν εὕρῃ ὁ οἰκέτης if a slave brings in **earnings,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Ath.” 22.}}
**II.4** **practising, exercising,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τῶν τεχνῶν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Gorgias” 450c; Κύπριδος “AP” 5.218 (Paul. Sil.); ἀκαθαρσίας “Epistle to the Ephesians” 4.19}}].
**II.5** **work of art, production,** τετράγωνος ἐ., of the Hermae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.27 (non legit Scholia); τῶν τειχῶν αἱ ἐργασίαι}} the fortification **works,** {{Refs|[prev\. author] 7.6.}}
**II.6** literary **execution,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἐ. ποιητική 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.11}}; **elaboration** of a topic, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.24.}}
**II.7** **production** of a play, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Arg. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Oxy.” 1235.108.}}
**III** **guild** or **company** of workmen, ἡ ἐ. τῶν βαφέων{{Refs|Judeich “Altertümer von Hierapolis” 50}}; ἐριοπλυτῶν {{Refs|[prev. work] 40; ἐ. θρεμματική uncertain meaning, [prev. work]227.}}

2040:
** ἐργᾰτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**workman, Hermes** {{NT|17.5 (from Delos), “NT.Mat.10.10”}}, etc.; especially **one who works the soil, husbandman,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|γῆς ἐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.109, 5.6; οἱ ἐ. οἱ περὶ γεωργίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.32}}: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 859, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “El.” 75}}, etc.: also with _substantive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἐ. ἀνήρ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.50; οὑργάτης λεώς}} the **country**-folk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 632}}; of animals, βοῦς ἐ. **a working** ox, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 39, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 563; ἐ. σφῆκες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627b32}}; also ἐ. θαλάττης, of a fisher, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.11; ἐ. λίθων}} a stone-**mason,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 2.}}
**b** in the religious sense, {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.15, “NT.2Cor.11.13” (_plural_).}}
**2** _adjective_ **hard-working, strenuous,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. στρατηγός Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.18; σώφρων κἀ. Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 611}}]; opposed to ἀργός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthd.” 281c; φειδωλὸς καὶ ἐ. [prev\. author] “R.” 554a}}.
**II** **one who practises** an art, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τῶν ἐν πολέμῳ Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.4}}]; ἐ. δίκης, of a judge, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 128: _absolutely_}}, **practitioner** in some special branch of surgery, e.g. lithotomy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. ἄνδρες 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Jusj.”}}
**III** **doer,** [{{NT+6th c.BC+|ἄσημος οὑργ. τις ἤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 252; τῶν καλῶν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 2.1.27; ἀνομίας Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “1 Maccabees” 3.6; τῆς ἀδικίας NT.Luke.13.27}}].
**IV** **producer,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|τῶν ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ γινομένων 1st c.AD: Heraclitus 75; [Αἰὼν] θείας φύσεως ἐ. “SIG” 1125.12 (from Eleusis).}}
**V** a sort of **capstan** or **windlass,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 58.12, 1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 10.2.7, +others, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.1.}}

2041:
** ἔργον**,
_Doric dialect_ **ϝέργον** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 4.800 (6th c.BC)}}, Elean **ϝάργον** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), τό: (ἔρδω, O 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **weorc** (_neuter_) 'work', Avest. **var[schwa]za**-):— **work,** [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.436, etc.; ἔ. οὐδὲν ὄνειδος, ἀεργίη δέ τ᾽ ὄνειδος {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 311; πλεόνων δέ τε ἔ. ἄμεινον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.412; ἔ. ἐποίχεσθαι 6.492; νῦν ἔπλετο ἔ. ἅπασι 12.271}}]; especially in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἄλλος ἄλλοισιν..ἐπιτέρπεται ἔργοις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.228; ἐπὶ ἔργα τράποντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.422; ἔργων παύσασθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.683}}; τὰ σ᾽ αὐτῆς ἔργα κόμιζε see to thine own **tasks,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.490: especially in the following relations}}
**1** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” mostly of}}**works** or **deeds** of war, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|πολεμήϊα ἔ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.338, +others, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.116; ἔργον μάχης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.522; alone, ἀτελευτήτῳ ἐπὶ ἔργῳ 4.175, compare 539; ὑπέσχετο δὲ μέγα ἔργον 13.366; ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ δ᾽ ἔργον ἐτύχθη ἀργαλέον 4.470; later, ἔργον.. Ἄρης κρινεῖ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Septem contra Thebas” 414}}]; ἐν τῷ ἔ. during the **action,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, compare 7.71; τὸ ἐν Πλαταιαῖς ἔ. Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Menexenus” 241c; τῶν πρότερον ἔ. μέγιστον ἐπράχθη τὸ Μηδικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23}}]; ἔργου ἔχεσθαι to engage in **battle,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 49.}}
**2** of peaceful **contests,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κρατεῖν ἔ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.85; ἔργου ἔχεσθαι [prev\. author] “P.” 4.233}}; also ἔργα θῆκε κάλλιστ᾽ ἀμφὶ κόμαις placed [the reward of] noble **deeds** about his hair, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “O.” 13.38.}}
**3** of **works of industry,**
**3.a** of **tillage, tilled lands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἀνδρῶν πίονα ἔ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.283, etc.; ἔργ᾽ ἀνθρώπων 16.392, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.259; βροτῶν 10.147}}; οὔτε βοῶν οὔτ᾽ ἀνδρῶν φαίνετο ἔργα {{Refs|[prev. work] 98; ἔργα alone, 16.140, etc.; Ἔργα καὶ Ἡμέραι}} —the title of Hesiod's work; πατρώϊα ἔ. their father's **lands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.22; οὔτ᾽ ἐπὶ ἔργα..ἴμεν}} will neither go to our **farms,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 127, compare 252; Ἰθάκης..ἔργα}} **the tilled lands** of Ithaca, {{Refs|14.344; ἀμφὶ.. Τιταρησσὸν ἔργ᾽ ἐνέμοντο}} inhabited **lands,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.751; τὰ τῶν Μυσῶν ἔ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36; so later, “PBaden” 40.5 (2nd c.AD)}}: generally, **property, wealth, possessions,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|θεὸς δ᾽ ἐπὶ ἔργον ἀέξῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.65, compare 15.372.}}
**3.b** of women's **work,** weaving, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.390, etc.; ἀμύμονα ἔ. ἰδυίας [prev. work] 128; ἔργα ἐργάζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.422, 20.72}}.
**3.c** of other **occupations,** θαλάσσια ἔ. fishing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.67; a seaman's life, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.614: periphrastic, δαιτὸς..ἔργα}} **works** of feasting, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.228; φιλοτήσια ἔ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.246; ἔργα γάμοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.429; ἔργα Κυπρογενοῦς 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 26; Ἀφροδίτης “hymnus ad Venerem” 1; also τέκνων ἐς ἔ. Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 1207}}]: _absolutely_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔργον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 17.1, “AP” 12.209 (_plural_, 2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus, assuming variant); also ἔργα ἰσχύος καὶ τάχους Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12}}]; φίλα ἔργα μελίσσαις, of flowers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.42}}; of **mines,** etc., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔ. ἀργυρεῖα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “de Vectigalibus” 4.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.167, etc.; ἔργα πίσσια uncertain reading in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 21.}}]
**4** **deed, action,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔργ᾽ ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.338; θέσκελα ἔ. Τρώων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.130; ἀήσυλα ἔ. 5.876}}; καρτερά, ἀεικέα ἔ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 872, 22.395; παλίντιτα, ἄντιτα ἔ., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.379, 17.51; ἔργα ἀποδέκνυσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Alc.” 1.119e, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.52; opposed to ἔπος}} **deed,** not word (see. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔπος 11.1); opposed to μῦθος, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.443, 19.242, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” 1080 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; opposed to λόγος, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 358, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alc.” 339; ἔργῳ, opposed to λόγῳ, frequently in _Attic dialect_, etc., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, etc.: so in _plural_, λόγῳ μὲν..τοῖσι δ᾽ ἔργοισιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 782, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “fragment” 360.13; λόγοισιν εἴτ᾽ ἔργοισιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 517}}; opposed to ῥήματα, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “OC” 873; opposed to ὄνομα, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “IA” 128 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.78, 89; in many phrases, πέπρακται τοὔργον Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 75, compare “Ag.” 1346; χωρῶ πρὸς ἔργον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 116}}]; τὸ μὲν ἐνθύμημα χαρίεν.., τὸ δὲ ἔ. ἀδύνατον its **execution,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 3.5.12; ἐν ἔργῳ χέρνιβες ξίφος τε}} ready for **action,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “IT” 1190; ἡ κατάρα ὑπὸ τοῦ δαίμονος εἰς ἔ. ἤγετο 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.228b}}.
**II** **thing, matter,** πᾶν ἔ...ὑπείξομαι in every **point,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.294; ἃ Ζεὺς μήδετο ἔ. 2.38, etc.; πάρος τάδε ἔ. γενέσθαι 6.348, etc.; ὅπως ἔσται τάδε ἔ. 2.252, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.78, etc.; μέμνημαι τόδε ἔ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.527; ἄκουε τοὔργον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1157, compare “OT” 847, “Aj.” 466}}; in bad sense, **mischief, trouble,** of disease, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|αἰτίη τοῦ ἔ. 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.9}}; μέγα ἔ. a serious **matter,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.663, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3.}}
**II.2** μέγα ἔ., like{μέγα χρῆμα}, χερμάδιον λάβε χειρὶ Τυδεΐδης, μέγα ἔ. a monstrous **thing,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.303, compare 20.286; φυλόπιδος μέγα ἔ.}} a **mighty** call to arms, {{Refs|16.208.}}
**III** _passive_, **that which is wrought** or **made, work,** οἷ᾽ ἐπιεικὲς ἔργ᾽ ἔμεν ἀθανάτων, of the arms of Achilles, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.22; ἔ. Ἡφαίστοιο}} metal-**work,** [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.617; πέπλοι.., ἔργα γυναικῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.289, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.97, compare 10.223; ὕφασμα, σῆς ἔ. χερός Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 231; κολεόν..λώτινον ἔ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.45}}]; of a wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Av.” 1125}}; of a statue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 3.10.7: in _plural_}}, of siege-**works,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ἔ. καὶ μηχαναί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.3.6}}; of a **machine,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Poliorc.” 157.4, +others, 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 15.2}}, +others; of public **buildings,** {{Refs|Mon.Anc.Gr. 18.20}}; of an author's **works,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; τὸ περὶ ψυχῆς ἔργον Ἀριστοτέλους “AP” 11.354.8 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**III.2** **result of work, profit** or **interest,** ἔργον [χρημάτων] **interest** or **profit** on money, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.42, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10.}}
**IV** special phrases:
**IV.1** ἔργον ἐστί,
**IV.1.a** with _genitive_ _person_, it is his **business,** his **proper work,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἀνδρῶν τόδ᾽ ἐστὶν ἔ. Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 673; ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἔ. ἀγαθοῦ πολίτου Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Gorgias” 517c}}]; of things, φραδέος νόου ἔργα τέτυκται it is **a matter** (which calls) for a wary mind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.354}}; **function,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἅπερ νεῶν ἄμεινον πλεουσῶν ἔργα ἐστίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; οὐ θερμότητος ἔργον ψύχειν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 335d}}]; τοῦτο ἑκάστου ἔ. ὃ ἂν ἢ μόνον τι ἢ κάλλιστα τῶν ἄλλων ἀπεργάζηται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 353a; ἔργα τοῦ ἐγκεφάλου _functions,_ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.518}}: with _dative_ _person_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|οἷς τοῦτο ἔ. ἦν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.36, compare 6.3.27: with the possessive _pronoun_, σὸν ἔ. [ἐστί]}}] with _infinitive_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” 635; ἐμὸν τόδ᾽ ἔ. κρῖναι [prev\. author] “Eu.” 734; σὸν ἔ., θῦε θεοῖς Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 862; ὑμέτερον ἐντεῦθεν ἔ. [prev\. author] “Pax” 426}}]: with _Article_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|νῦν ἡμέτερον τὸ ἔ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.1}}.
**IV.1.b** with _genitive_ _of things_, **there is need** of.., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τί δῆτα τόξων ἔ.; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 39; πολλῆς φυλακῆς ἔ. [ἐστί] Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 537d}}]: especially with _negative_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|οὐδὲν..ὀδόντων ἔ. ἐστ᾽ Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Pax” 1310; οὐ δόλου νῦν ἔ. [prev\. author] “Pl.” 1158, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hipp.” 911: with _dative_ _person_, ἐπέδρης μὴ εἶναι ἔ. τῇ στρατιῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17}}]: with _Article_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οὐκ ἂν μακρῶν λόγων ἡμῖν τόδ᾽ εἴη τοὔργον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1373}}: with a _participle_ added, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|οὐδὲν ἦν ἔ. αὐτοῦ κατατείνοντος Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Publicola” 13}}]: also with _infinitive_, οὐδὲν ἔ. ἑστάναι there is no **use in** standing still, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lys.” 424, compare “Av.” 1308; οὐδὲν ἔ. ταῦτα θρηνεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 852, compare 12.}}
**IV.1.c** with _infinitive_, **it is hard work, difficult** to do, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πολὺ ἔ. ἂν εἴη διεξελθεῖν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 4.6.1; πολὺ ἔ. ἦν τῷ νομοθέτῃ πάντα γράφειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.7; ἔ. ἐστὶν εἰ ἐροῦμεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.51; ἔ. εὑρεῖν πρόφασιν Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 76; also μέγα ἔ. ταῖς..ἐπιθυμίαις καλῶς χρῆσθαι Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 187e; χαλεπὸν ἔ. διαιρεῖν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ranae” 1100}}] (Lyric poetry): also in _genitive_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πλείονος ἔ. ἐστὶ..μαθεῖν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Euthyphro” 14b}}]: rarely with a _participle_, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|οὐδὲν ἔ. μαχομένῳ Philippides Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 15.3}}]; ἔ. [ἐστί] with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **it can scarcely happen** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἔ. ἅμα πάντας ὀργισθῆναι καὶ ἁμαρτεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a35}}.
**IV.2** ἔργον παρασχεῖν τινί give one **trouble,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 523, compare “AP” 9.161 (Marc. argument, punning on Hesiod's Ἔργα); ἔργον ἔχειν}} take **trouble,** with _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 8.4.6}}; with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Mem.” 2.10.6.}}
**IV.3** ἔ. γίγνεσθαι τῆς νόσου to be its **victim,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ἄτολμοι}; κτεινόμενος ὑμέτερον ἔ. εἰμί Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Eumenes” 17; τῆς ὑμετέρας γέγονεν ἔ. ὀλιγωρίας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 29}}].
**IV.4** ἔ. ποιεῖσθαί τι to make a matter **one's business, attend to** it, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 232a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hier.” 9.10; so ἐν ἔργῳ τίθεσθαι Aelianus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Varia Historia” 4.15}}].
**V** ={ἐργασία} III, {{Refs|τὸ ἔ. βαφέων “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3498 (from Thyatira).}}

2042:
** ἐρεθίζω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-ιζέμεν** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.5}}: _imperfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἠρέθιζον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 965}} (Lyric poetry), _Epic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐρ- 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.419}}: _future_ **-ίσω** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.385}}, **-ιῶ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 2, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.4.2}}: _aorist 1_ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἠρέθισα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.72; poetry ἐρ- Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 183}}](Lyric poetry), _infinitive_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐρεθίξαι “AP” 12.37 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus)}}: _perfect_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἠρέθικα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.37}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ ἠρεθίσθην, _participle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐρεθισθείς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.40, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.57}}: _perfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἠρέθισμαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} (see. below), etc.: (ἐρέθω):—**rouse to anger, rouse to fight,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.32; κερτομίοις ἐπέεσσι 5.419}}; κύνας τ᾽ ἄνδρας τε, of a lion, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|17.658; ἐ. τοὺς Πέρσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146; φιλαύλους τ᾽ ἠρ. Μούσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 965 (Lyric poetry); ὥσπερ σφηκιὰν ἐ. τινά Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Lysistrata” 475; χεῖρον..ἐρεθίσαι γραῦν ἢ κύνα Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 802}}]; πὺξ ἐ. **challenge to** a boxing-match, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.2}}; **provoke to curiosity,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|μητέρα σήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.45}}: generally, **excite, chafe,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|φρένας ἐ. φόβος Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 183}}](Lyric poetry); of physical **irritation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 2; βῆχες βραχέα -ουσαι}} **causing** brief **irritation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Aph.” 4.54: _metaphorically_, ἐ. πλανάτας χοροῖσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 148}} (Lyric poetry); ἐ. μάγαδιν **to touch** it, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Telestes Lyricus 4; φλόγα 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.9; τὸ φονικὸν καὶ θηριῶδες Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.822c}}]; **incite** to rivalry, {{NT|NT.2Cor.9.2:—}}_passive_, **to be provoked, excited,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ὑπό τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “V.” 1104; ἠρεθισμένος _under provocation, Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 574; ὀργῇ χεῖρας -ισμένας 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 8.3}}]; of love, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τοῖς νέοισιν -ισμένος 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 30}}; of fire, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|φέψαλος.. -όμενος..ῥιπίδι Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 669 (Lyric poetry); αἰθὴρ -έσθω βροντῇ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 1045}}] (_anapaest meter_); πνεῦμα ἠρεθισμένον, of one who has run till he is out of breath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1119; ἕλκος ἠρεθισμένον _irritated,_ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 27, compare 31, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.81.6; ὀσμὴ -ισμένη 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 75.9; ἐπὶ τὴν ὕβριν ἠρεθίσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 22}}.
**II** _absolutely_, **to be quarrelsome** or **perverse,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.359.}}

2043:
** ἐρείδω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔρειδον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.131}}: _future_ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐρείσω 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 234, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Or.” 17(15).10}} codices: _aorist 1_ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἤρεισα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1236, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 254e, “Ti.” 91e; _Boeotian dialect_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _sing._ εἴρισε 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 1.32}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔρεισα (ἐπ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.269}}: _perfect_ ἤρεικα (συν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Morb.Sacr.” 7, (προσ-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.60.8; but ἐρήρεικα 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.84, (προσ-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aem.” 19:}} —_middle_, _future_ ἐρείσομαι (ἀπ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr.” 885b29, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.25}}: _aorist 1_ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἠρεισάμην {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 362, (ἀπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 508d; _Epic dialect_ ἐρ- 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.309}}]:—_passive_, {{Refs|3}} _future_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐρηρείσεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐρείσθην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.145}}: _perfect_ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|ἐρήρεισμαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.152, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 78 (but _2nd pers._ _sing._ ἠρήρεισθα 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 94 is from ἀραρίσκω); also ἤρεισμαι Ti. _Locrian dialect_ 98e (ἐρήρ- [prev. work] 97e), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.12, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.25.5; _Ionic dialect_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _plural_}} _perfect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐρηρέδαται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.284, 329, _Epic dialect_ ἐρήρεινται 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.320}}: _pluperfect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἠρήρειστο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.136}}; 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _plural_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐρηρέδατο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.95, ἠρήρειντο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1398:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses the augment only in ἠρήρειστο, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Sc.” 362 in ἠρείσατο.—_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and poet. Verb, also found in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} and later Prose:—**cause to lean, prop,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|δόρυ..πρὸς τεῖχος ἐρείσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.112; θρόνον πρὸς κίονα μακρὸν ἐρείσας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.66; πύργῳ ἔπι προὔχοντι..ἀσπίδ᾽ ἐρείσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.97}}; [νέκυας] ἀλλήλοισιν ἐ. **piling** them **against** each other, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.450; ἐρείσατε..πλευρὸν ἀμφιδέξιον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1112}}; πρὸς στέρν᾽ ἐρείσας (i.e. τοὺς παῖδας) [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “HF” 1362, compare “Ba.” 684; τὰ ἰσχία πρὸς τὴν γῆν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedrus” 254e; ἐ. τινὰ εἰς ἕδραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 603; τὰς κεφαλὰς εἰς γῆν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Timaeus” 91e; ἐς χεῖρας ἐ. τι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.104; ἐ. τὴν κεφαλὴν ἐπὶ γῆς Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Timaeus” 43e; τὸ γόνυ κατὰ τοῦ ἰσχίου Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Flamininus” 20; ῥόῳ ἔνι κάλπιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1234}}]: generally, **fix firmly, plant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἄγκυραν χθονί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.51; εἰς γῆν ἐ. ὄμμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1123, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Or.” 17(15).10; ἐπὶ χθονὸς ὄμματ᾽ 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.784}}; ἐ. πόδας ἐς βένθος **plant** the foot **firm,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1010: _metaphorically_, ἐ. τὰν γνώμαν}} **fix** one's mind **firmly** on a thing, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.61.}}
**2** **prop up, support, stay,** ἀσπὶς ἄρ᾽ ἀσπίδ᾽ ἔρειδε, κόρυς κόρυν, ἀνέρα δ᾽ ἀνήρ, of close ranks of men-atarms, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.131; ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδος ἀσπίδ᾽ ἔρεισον 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.31; πέλτην ἐρεῖσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 487; κίον᾽ οὐρανοῦ τε καὶ χθονὸς ὤμοιν ἐ. Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 352}}].
**3** **press hard, attack,** τινα [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.32: obscene meaning, ἐ. γυναῖκα Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ecclesiazusae” 616, “fragment” 74.}}]
