**III.2** **to be fixed firm, planted,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔγχος διὰ θώρηκος ἠρήρειστο _had been fixed,_ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.358, etc.; λᾶε ἐρηρέδαται _stand firmly fixed,?? 23.329; θρόνοι περὶ τοῖχον ἐρηρέδατ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.95; ἁ γᾶ ἐρήρεισται ἐπὶ τᾶς αὐτᾶς ῥοπᾶς Ti.Locrian dialect 97e}}: _absolutely_, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|δίκας ἐρείδεται πυθμήν _is set firm,?? Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 646}}] (Lyric poetry); opposed to πλανᾶσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “GA” 720a12; ἐρηρεικός}} of a bone **stuck** in the throat, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.84.}}
**III.3** ἐρείδεσθαι ναυαγίαις **to be driven ashore** in shipwreck, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.36.}}
**IV** _middle_,
**IV.1** in _reciprocal_ sense, **struggle** one with another, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.735 (variant{ἐρίζεσθον}).}}]
**IV.2** with _accusative_, **support** or **set firmly for oneself,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|πλησίον ἠρείσαντο καρήατα Simonides Lyricus [{4-5th c.BC}] 172; βάκτρῳ δ᾽ ἐρείδου..στίβον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 743; ἐπὶ γαῖαν ἴχνος “AP” 12.84 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ἐπὶ τοίχῳ λίθον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.49; ἐπὶ χειρὶ παρειήν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1160; χεῖρας σκηπανίῳ “AP” 6.83 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus); ἐπὶ σκίπωνος τὸ γῆρας [prev. work] 7.457 (from Aristo); ἐς πόλον ἐκ γαίης μῆτιν ἐ.}}] **to raise one's** thoughts.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.782 (Paul. Sil.).}}

2045:
** ἐρευν-άω**,
also **ἐρευν-ίω** {{Refs|“GDI” 5075.35 (from Crete)}}, and **ἐραυνάω** (which see):— **seek** or **search for, search after, track,** ἴχνι᾽ ἐρευνῶντες κύνες ἤϊσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.436; μετ᾽ ἀνέρος ἴχνι᾽ ἐρευνῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.321; τεύχε᾽ ἐ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.180; τὴν σοφὴν εὐβουλίαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1038; θεῶν βουλεύματ᾽ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61; νεκρούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1318; κακούργους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12; ἄν τινα οἴωμαι σοφὸν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23b. compare 41b; τὸ γραμματεῖον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.61}}; ὧν..ἂν θεὸς χρείαν ἐρευνᾷ the things whereof he **seeks after** the use, i.e. whatever things he finds serviceable, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 725.}}
**2** **search, explore** a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.δ᾽, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “Vit.Hippocr.” 3}}; τεναγέων ῥοάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.24; ὄρος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.221; τοὺς ὑπόπτους τῶν τόπων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 17: _absolutely_, εὑρήσεις ἐρευνῶν}} thou wilt find **by searching,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.113, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 268; εἰσβάντες εἰς τὸ πλοῖον ἠρεύνων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.29.}}
**3** **inquire after,** φάτιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 662 (Lyric poetry); παίδων ἐρευνῶν σπέρμ᾽ ὅπως γένοιτό μοι [prev. author] “Med.” 669}}; **examine into** a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1084 (_anapaest meter_),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200e, +others:}} —also in _middle_, διάνοια πᾶσαν φύσιν -ωμένη{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 174a; οἰκημάτιον 2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 2.10.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **seek** to do, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.45.}}

2046:
** ἐρῶ**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ of εἴρω (B), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.9, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 45, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 61, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ἐρέω** (later as _present_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 484, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.400a, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.878, +others)}}; _optative_ ἐροίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.14, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 1.87:}}_imperfect_ ἤρεον (variant{εἴρεον}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.2.9}}: _perfect_ _active_ εἴρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 821, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 558, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ εἰρήκειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.184d}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ εἴρημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.363, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 13, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰρέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.81}}, _contraction_ εἰρῆται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 811.17 (Oropus, 4th c.BC)}}; _participle_ εἰρημένος, Cretan dialect ϝερημένος {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 2.509, argument ϝεϝρημένος “Schwyzr” 98 (from Mycenae)}}: _pluperfect_ εἴρητο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.540, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.27, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ _passive_ ἐρρήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 664d, later ἐρρέθην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 11b12, +others; Ionic εἰρέθην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.77, 156}}: _future_ ῥηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.73, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.53: more frequently εἰρήσομαι}} mostly in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ **-ήσεται**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.795, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).59, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1276, etc.}}; _participle_ **-όμενος**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 53. — 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only}}_future_ ἐρέω, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_, with _participle_ εἰρημένος, _aorist_ _participle_ ῥήθείς in the phrase ἐπὶ ῥηθέντι δικαίῳ (see. below), and _future_ _passive_ —The place of the _present_ εἴρω (which see) is supplied by φημί, λέγω or ἀγορεύ; εἶπον serves as _aorist_ (ϝερε-: ϝρη-, compare ῥήτρα, ῥητός, Latin _verbum,_ Engl. **word.**)
**I** I **will say** or **speak,** with _accusative_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.297: _absolutely_, οὐδὲ πάλιν ἐρέει}} he **will say** nothing against it, {{Refs|9.56}}; ἐν δ᾽ ὑμῖν ἐρέω among you,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 528, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.378}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_, ἐρεῖν τι πρός τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520a (_active_), 595b, “Tht.” 179a (both _passive_)}}; τι περί τινος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 29d, etc.}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **speak of,** κακῶς ἐρεῖν τινα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 796, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 705: and with double _accusative_, ἐρεῖ δέ μ᾽..τάδε [prev. work] 954, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48a}}:—_passive_, μῦθος..εἰρημένος ἔστω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.524; εἰρημένα μυθολογεύειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.453}}; λίαν εἰρημένος too **true,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1031}}; ἐπὶ ῥηθέντι δικαίῳ after justice **has been pronounced,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.414}}; ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον εἰρήσθω περί τινος **let** this **suffice,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1117b21.}}
**II** I **will tell, proclaim,** ἔπος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.419, etc.}}; Ἠὼς..Ζηνὶ φόως ἐρέουσα **announce** it,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|2.49; ἐρέω τιν᾽ ὑμῖν αἶνον 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89.1.}}
**II.2** **tell, order,** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.6, etc.}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.3.6}}:—_passive_, εἴρητο συλλέγεσθαι τὸν στρατόν **orders had been given..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26, etc.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be mentioned,** οὗτοι μὲν οἱ παραθαλάσσιοι.. εἰρέαται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.181, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 393b27.}}
**III.2** **to be specified, agreed, promised,** εἰρημένος μισθός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 370, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.23}}; εἰρημένον, _absolutely_, **when it had been agreed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140}}; κὰ (τ) τὰ ϝεϝρημένα {{Refs|“Schwyzer” [same place]}}

2047:
** ἐρημ-ία**, ἡ,
**I** of places, **a solitude, desert, wilderness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 2, etc.; ἡ Σκυθῶν ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 704; ἀφίκετ᾽ εἰς ἐ. [prev. author] “Lys.” 787}}; ἕρπει εἰς τὰς ἐρημίας to **solitary places,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610b24, etc.}}
**II** as a state or condition, **solitude, loneliness,** ἐρημίαν ἄγειν, to keep **alone,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 50; μονάδ᾽ ἔχουσ᾽ ἐ. [prev. author] “Ba.” 609 (troch.); ἐρημίας τυχών [prev. author] “El.” 510; ἐν ἐρημίᾳ ἐλοιδοροῦντο 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4}}; of persons, **isolation, destitution,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 957, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.25; πολλὴν ἡμῶν ἐ. καταγνόντες 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.2}}; δι᾽ ἐρημίαν from **being left alone,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, compare 3.67; ἐρημίας ἐπειλημμένοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.27; εὑρετικὸν εἶναί φασι τὴν ἐ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 39.}}
**II.b** of places, **desolation,** ἐρημίᾳ δοῦναί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 97}}; ἐρημία..πόλιν ὅταν λάβῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 26; ἀτριβὴς ὑπ᾽ ἐρημίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **want of, absence,** φίλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.14}}; ἀρσένων, βροτῶν, ἀνδρῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1017, “Ba.” 875 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.102; λύχνων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1484 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; δι᾽ ἐρημίας πολεμίων πορευόμενος **without finding** any enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.21}}; τὴν ἐ. τῶν κωλυσόντων ὁρῶν seeing that there would be **none** to hinder him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.49}}; ἐ. σώματος, ={κενόν}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.26}}; even ἐ. κακῶν **freedom from** evil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1157.}}

2048:
** ἐρῆμος**, ον,
_feminine_ ἐρήμη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.270, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1719 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 739, “Tr.” 530 (Lyric poetry), and in the phrase δίκη ἐρήμη (see. infr. III)}}: _Attic dialect_ **ἔρημος**, ον, _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|2.938}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.1, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.118}}:—**desolate, lonely, solitary,**
**1** of places, ἐς νῆσον ἐρήμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.270; χῶρος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.520}}; τὰ ἐ. τῆς Λιβύης the **desert parts..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17}}; ἡ ἐρῆμος (i.e. χώρα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.18; ἡ ἐρήμη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.48: _plural_, [prev. work] 3.26}}; **empty,** πνύξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 20.}}
**2** of personsor animals, τὰ δ᾽ ἐρῆμα φοβεῖται (i.e. the sheep), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.140; ξέρξην ἔ. μολεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 734 (troch.); ἧσθαι δόμοις ἔ. [prev. author] “Ag.” 862; πόρτις ἐρήμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 530 (Lyric poetry); ἔ. κἄφιλος [prev. author] “Ph.” 228; τὸν θεὸν ἔ. ἀπολιπόντε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 447}}; frequently of poor, friendless persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.15, etc.}}; ἐρημότεροι, opposed to δυνατώτεροι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11; οὐκ ὢν οὔτε τῶν ἐρημοτάτων οὔτε τῶν ἀπόρων κομιδῇ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.111; εἰς ὀρφανὰ καὶ ἔ. ὑβρίζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927c; ὄρνιθες}}**solitary, not gregarious,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 63: _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, ἔρημα κλαίω I weep **in solitude,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 775}}; ἔρημον ἐμβλέπειν to look **vacantly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 456.}}
**3** of conditions, πλάνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1114.}}
**II** with _genitive_, **reft of, void** or **destitute of,** [χώρη] ἐ. πάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32; ἀνθρώπων [prev. author] 4.17, compare 18; ἀνδρῶν [prev. author] 6.23, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 57; Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65; στέγαι φίλων ἔ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1405; Πειραιᾶ ἔ. ὄντα νεῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.96}}; τῇ ἦν ἐρημότατον τῶν πολεμίων (i.e. τὸ τεῖχος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.118; [τὰ γεγραμμένα] ἀπόντος τοῦ γράψαντος ἔρημα τοῦ βοηθήσοντός ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 1.3; θεῶν ἔρημα εἶναι πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908c.}}
**II.2** of persons, **bereft of,** συμμάχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160; πατρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1719 (Lyric poetry); πατρὸς ἢ μητρός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927d; πρὸς φίλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 919}}; so ἔ. οἶκος a house **without heirs,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.31.}}
**II.3** with no bad sense, **wanting, without,** ἐσθὴς ἐρῆμος ὅπλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.63}}; **free from,** ἀνδρῶν κακῶν ἔρημος πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 862e.}}
**III** ἐρήμη, rarely ἔρημος (with or more commonly without γραφή, δίκη, δίαιτα), ἡ, an **undefended** action, **in which one party does not appear,** and judgement goes against him **by default,** ἤλπιζε.. τὴν γραφὴν..ἐρήμην ἔσεσθαι would be **undefended,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.7; ἐρήμῃ δίκῃ θάνατον καταγιγνώσκειν τινός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61}}; δίκην εἷλον ἐρήμην I got **judgement by default,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.81; ἐρήμην αὐτὸν λαβόντες..εἷλον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.18}}; τὴν ἔρημον δεδωκότα having given **it by default** in one's favour, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.85}}; ἔρημον ὦφλε δίκην he let it go **by default,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]87,compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13; ἐρήμην τινὸς καταγνῶναι τὴν δίαιταν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.33; ἐρήμην καταδιαιτῆσαί τινος [prev. author] 40.17; γενομένης ἐρήμου κατὰ Μειδίου Test. cited in [same work] 21.93}}; ἐρήμην κατηγορεῖν to accuse **in a case where there was no defence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87}}; ἐρήμην or ἐξ ἐρήμης κρατεῖν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 40, “JTr.” 25; ἁλῶναι [prev. author] “Tox.” 11, etc.}}
**III.2** **unclaimed, vacant,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.4, “EN” 1125b17, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.61.}}
**III.3** for ἐρήμας τρυγᾶν see entry τρυγάω.

2049:
** ἐρημ-όω**,
**strip bare, desolate, lay waste,** ἱερὰ θεῶν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58; τὴν χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.21; πλοῦτον LXX.Sir.21.4; ὁ κτίζων καὶ ἐρημῶν θεός “POsl.” 1.105}}:—_passive_, ἐρημωθείσης Κρήτης {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.171; πόλεις ἠρημώθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23; μιᾷ ὥρᾳ ἠρημώθη ὁ τοσοῦτος πλοῦτος NT.Rev.18.17.}}
**II** **bereave** one **of** a thing, with _double_ _accusative_, ἐ. τινὰ εὐφροσύνας μέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.97: with _accusative_ et _genitive_, ἀνδρῶν ἐ. ἑστίαν [prev. author] “I.” 4(3).17}}; ἐ. ναυβατῶν ἐρετμά **to leave** the oars **without** men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1609}}; ἑαυτὸν ἐρημοῖς (i.e. φίλων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus39}}:—_passive_, **to be bereft of,** ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.164; συμμάχων [prev. author] 7.174; Μίλητος Μιλησίων ἠρήμωτο [prev. author] 6.22; ἄρσενος θρόνου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 260; πατρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 805; τὰ ἐρημούμενα φυλακῆς}}**left without,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Eq. Mag.” 4.18.}}
