2083:
** ἑταῖρ-ος**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ also **ἕγᾰρος**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Cleobulus Lyricus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.93, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 988 (Lyric poetry)}}, ὁ:— **comrade, companion,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} especially of the followers of a chief, **comrades-in-arms,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.179, +others}}; **messmate,** {{Refs|17.577}}; **fellowslave,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.407, +others: joined with ἀνήρ, 8.584, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18}}; later, as a term of address, φίλ᾽ ἑταῖρε {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 753, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482a}}; ὦταῖρε Scol. cited in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1238, cf. NT.Matt.20.13, +others: with _genitive_, δᾳιτὸς ἑταῖρε}} **partner** of my feast, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 436; νυκτὸς ἑ. [prev. work] 290}}; πόσιος καὶ βρώσιος ἑταῖροι mess**mates,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 115; ἑ. ἐν πρήγματι [prev. author] 116.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of things, ἐσθλὸς ἑταῖρος, of a fair wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.7, 12.149; φθόνος κενεοφρόνων ἑ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 212; γέλως ἑ. ὕβρεων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.622b: with _dative_, βίον..τὸν σοφοῖς ἕταρον “Anthologia Graeca” 7.470 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** **pupil, disciple,** e.g. of Socrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.1, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274a28; Λεύκιππος καὶ ὁ ἑ. αὐτοῦ Δημόκριτος [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 985b4}}: _plural_, **fellow-pupils,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.45.}}
**4** of political **partisans** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἑταιρεία 1.2), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.43, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48}}; οἱ περὶ αὐτὸν ἑ. his **club-mates,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.20.}}
**5** **members of a religious guild**, {{Refs|“OGI” 573.1 (Cilicia, Jewish).}}
**6** rarely of **lovers,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 912 (Lyric poetry).}}
**7** ἑταῖροι, οἱ, the **guards,** i.e. the **cavalry** of the Macedonian kings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217, Anaximen.Lamps. cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}} see at {πεζέταιρος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.16.11, etc.}}; to be distinguished from the king's immediate **retinue** (compare above {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1), Theopomp. [same place], 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.12.6, +others}}; of the {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Comites” of the Roman Emperor, Βαρβίλλῳ τῷ ἐμῷ ἑτέρῳ (sic) “PLond.” 1912.105 (Epist. Claudii), compare “SIG” 798.6 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD, _plural_).}}
**8** as _adjective_, **associate of,** τὸ ἐπιθυμητικὸν ἡδονῶν ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439d: _superlative_, τοῖς σεαυτοῦ ἑταιροτάτοις}} your **closest companions,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 487d, compare “Phd.” 89e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 1.44}}; σαργῶν γένος πέτρῃσιν ἑ. **constant to** the rocks, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.267}}: _absolutely_, of animals, **gregarious,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “C.” 2.325.}}
**II** **ἑταίρα**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἑταίρη**, _Epic dialect_ **ἑτάρη** [ᾰ], ἡ, **companion,** Ἔρις..Ἄρεος..κασιγνήτη ἑτάρη τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.441; Λάτω καὶ Νιόβα μάλα μὲν φίλαι ἦσαν ἔ. 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 31, compare 11; φύζα, φόβου κρυόεντος ἑ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.2; φόρμιγξ.. ἣν ἄρα δαιτὶ θεοὶ ποίησαν ἑ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.271, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 478; Νίκην, ἣ χορικῶν ἐστιν ἑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 589; μιμητικὴ..τῷ ἐν ἡμῖν ἑ. καὶ φίλη ἐστί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 603b}}; Ποσειδάωνος ἑ., of a submerged city, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 101.}}
**II.2** **courtesan,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 149, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.567a, 571d, etc.}}; opposed to πόρνη (a common **prostitute**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.1}}; opposed to γαμετή, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|{5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 5; Ἀφροδίτη ἑ. Apollod.Hist. 17.}}]

2084:
** ἑτερό-γλωσσος**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἑτερόγλωττος**, ον, **of other** (i.e. **foreign) tongue**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.13.2, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.1.2}}; ἐν ἑτερογλώσσοις λαλεῖν **by men of foreign tongue**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.14.21, compare 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 26.2, Aq. 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 33.19.}}
**2** **of diverse tongues**, ζῷα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.406.}}

2087:
** ἕτερος**, α, ον,
only _Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ with **ἕ-**, _Doric dialect_ **ἅτερος** [ᾰ] {{Refs|“IG” 4.914.9 (Epid.), etc. (and _Attic dialect_ in crasis, see.below)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **ἄτερος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41.5, etc.}}:— but **ἅτερος** [ᾱ], _Attic dialect_ crasis for ὁ ἅτερος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 14.23 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, +others}}, _Ionic dialect_ **οὕτερος** (from ὁ ἕτ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ **ὥτερος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.36}}; _neuter_ θάτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 344, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.7, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ τοὔτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32: _plural_ ἅτεροι, for οἱ ἅτεροι, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1255a20; θάτερα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 345, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, etc.; _genitive_ θατέρου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 597, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ τοὐτέρου {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.113}}, _Doric dialect_ θατέρω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 94a, θωτέρω 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 71 (uncertain reading); _dative_ θατέρῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 778; _feminine_ _nominative_ ἡτέρα “IG” 22.1498.76, 1615.14,87 (4th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 497, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 85, 90 codices, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 82; _dative_ θητέρᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 782, “Tr.” 272, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 894, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1365, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus [prev. cited] (in Mss. sometimes θατέρᾳ)}}, _Ionic dialect_ τἠτέρῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 5.2.}} —Later _masculine_ and _feminine_ θάτερος, θατέρα, even with the _Article_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 846, Chrysipp. cited in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 82, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 590, 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Cynegirus” 4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Mort.” 26.1 (condemned in “Pseudol.” 29), “Geoponica” 14.20.2, etc.; τῶν θατέρων 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.83 P.}}; θάτερον _accusative_ _singular_ _masculine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” [849].}}
**I** **one** or **the other of two**, usually with _Article_ except in Poets; frequently of natural pairs, σκαιῇ (i.e. χειρὶ) ἔγχος ἔχων, ἑτέρηφι δὲ λάζετο πέτρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.734}}; τῇ ἑτέρῃ μὲν.. τῇ δ᾽ ἑτέρῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. 14.272, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.11}}; χειρὶ ἑτέρῃ with **one** hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.452, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.171 (but χεὶρ ἑτέρη}} commonly of the **left** hand, see infr. IV.I); ἑτέροιο διὰ κροτάφοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.502; χωλὸς δ᾽ ἕτερον πόδα 2.217, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 162, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.82; ἀμφότεραι αἱ γνάθοι, ἢ ἡ ἑτέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.9; ἐκκοπεὶς τὸν ἕ. τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.23; εἰς γόνυ θάτερον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.20}}; of pairs in general, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.258, etc.}}; τὴν ἑ. πύλην **one of the** two gates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156; ὁ ἕ. τῶν στρατηγῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43; τὸ ἕ. τοῖν δυοῖν τειχοῖν [prev. author] 7.24}}: frequently of alternatives presented, τῶνδε τὰ ἕ. ποιέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.126}}; ἑλοῦ γε θάτερ᾽, ἢ.. ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 345}}; τοῖνδ᾽ ἑλοῦ δυοῖν πότμοιν τὸν ἕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 952; δυοῖν ἀγαθοῖν τοῦ ἑτέρου τεύξεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28}}; δυοῖν θάτερα, ἢ.. ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187c; ὅταν δυοῖν καλοῖν θάτερον κάλλιον ᾖ, ἢ τῷ ἑτέρῳ τούτοιν ἢ ἀμφοτέροις ὑπερβάλλον κάλλιόν ἐστιν [prev. author] “Grg.” 475a}}: in _plural_, **one of two parties** or **sets**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.258}}; τῶν ἕτεροί γε παῖδα κλαύσονται **one set** of parents, either mine or thine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.210; δώῃ δ᾽ ἑτέροισί γε νίκην 7.292; ἑτέροισι δὲ κῦδος ἔδωκαν 13.303: frequently with _negative_, οὐδ᾽ ἕτεροι 11.71.}}
**I.2** in double clauses ἕτερος (in Prose always ὁ ἕτερος) is generally repeated; ἑ. μὲν δουρὶ.., τῷ δ᾽ ἑ. {{Refs|21.164}}; τὸν ἕ., ἕ. δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.266; ἕ. λευκόν, ἑτέρην δὲ μέλαιναν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.103, etc.: but sometimes omitted in one clause, [ἕτερος μὲν] κακῶν, ἕ. δὲ ἐάων 24.528, compare 7.420, “IG” 22.1388.46 (probably), etc.; ἡ μὲν.., ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.149, “IG” 12.76.50}}; ἕ..., ὁ δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.374; answered by ἄλλος, ἕτερον μὲν κεύθῃ ἐνὶ φρεσίν, ἄλλο δὲ εἴπῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.313, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.123}}; reversely ἄλλῳ ὀρχηστύν, ἑτέρῳ κίθαριν [ἔδωκε] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.731, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439b, “Tht.” 184e}}; τότε μὲν ἕτερα.., τότε δὲ ἄλλα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.116e}}; ὁ ἕτερος.., ὁ λοιπός.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.23}}; ἕτερα.., τὰ δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1454 (Lyric poetry); later μίαν μὲν.. ἑτέραν δέ 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 172.5; τὴν μίαν.. τὴν δ᾽ ἑτέρην “Anthologia Graeca” 9.680.}}
**I.3** repeated in the same clause, ἐξ ἑτέρων ἕτερ᾽ ἐστίν **one** building follows on **another**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.266}}; [ἀ] δ᾽ ἀτέρα τὰν ἀτέραν κύλιξ ὠθήτω let **one** cup push on **the other**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41.5}}; ἢ θάτερον δεῖ δυστυχεῖν ἢ θάτερον **one party** or **the other**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” [849]; ἕτεροι ἑτέρων ἠξίωσαν ἄρχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; ἕτερος ἀφ᾽ ἑτέρου θεραπείας ἀναπιμπλάμενοι ἔθνῃσκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 51; εἴ τίς τι ἕτερος ἑτέρου προφέρει [prev. author] 7.64; ξυμμειγνυμένων ἑτέρων ἑτέροις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 701}}; συμφορὰ ἑτέρα ἑτέρους πιέζει **one** calamity oppresses **one, another others**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 893 (Lyric poetry); ἑτέρᾳ δ᾽ ἕτερος ἕτερον ὄλβῳ καὶ δυνάμει παρῆλθεν [prev. author] “Ba.” 905, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 231 (_anapaest meter_); ἄλλη δ᾽ εἰς ἑτέρην ὀλυφύρετο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.250.}}
**I.4** ={δεύτερος}, **second**, ἡ μὲν.., ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρη.., ἡ δὲ τρίτη.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.352f, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.179, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.22}}; ἡ ἑ. πρότασις the **minor** premiss, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143b3: without _Article_, ἕ. τέρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57}}; προσαγορεύεις αὐτὰ ἑτέρῳ ὀνόματι you call them further by a **new** name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 13a; cf. IV. 1b.}}
**I.4.b** with Pronouns of quantity, ordinals, etc., τόσσοι δ᾽ αὖθ᾽ ἕτεροι ποταμοί as many **more**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 367}}; ἕτερον τοσοῦτον as much **again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}; ἑτέρου τοσούτου χρόνου for as long **again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.153}}; ἕ. τοιαῦτα **other things** of like kind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 191; ἑτέρων τοιῶνδε (i.e. ἀνθρώπων) ἄρχεις [prev. work] 207}}; τῷ αὐτῷ τρόπῳ.. τῷ ἑτέρῳ in the same way **over again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.127; ἄλλα τε τοιαῦθ᾽ ἕτερα μυρία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 333.4}}; χιλίας ἑτέρας [δραχμάς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.6}}; δεύτερον, τρίτον ἕ. δικαστήριον, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.71,74}}; ἕ. ἐγώ, of a friend, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.35 P.}}; ἕτεροι αὐτοί **second** selves, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161b28; εὕρηκε τὸν ἕ., τὸν σέ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 474.}}
**II** without _Article_, **another**, of many, with a sense of **difference**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.306, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 422, “Lys.” 66, etc.; ἕ.αὖ τις [prev. author] “Eq.” 949; ἕ. αὖ [prev. author] “Pax” 295, etc.; ἕτερα ἄττα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 188b; repeated ἑτέραν χἀτέραν τρικυμίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 536.8: with _negative_, οἷα οὐχ ἕτερα [ἐγένετο]}} such as none **like them** had happened, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23; ναυμαχία.. οἵα οὐχ ἑτέρα τῶν προτέρων [prev. author] 7.70; οὐδεμιᾶς ἥσσων μᾶλλον ἑτέρας [prev. work] 29 (assuming variant)}}; οὐχ ἕτερον ἀλλά.. **none other than**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.671b, compare “UPZ” 71.9 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.b** οἱ ἕ. **the rest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.169.}}
**II.c** ὁ ἑ. '**one's neighbour**', ἀγαπᾶν τὸν ἕ. {{NT|NT.Rom.13.6, cf. NT.Gal.6.4.}}
**III** **of another kind, different**, ἕ. δέ με θυμὸς ἔρυκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.302}}; τὸ μὲν ἕ., τὸ δὲ ἕ., i.e. they are **different**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97d, compare “R.” 346a; ἕ. τε καὶ ἀνόμοιον [prev. author] “Smp.” 186b; τὸ δὲ ταὐτὸν ἕ. ἀποφαίνειν καὶ τὸ θάτερον ταὐτόν [prev. author] “Sph.” 259d; ἕ. ἤδη ἦν καὶ οὐχ ὁ αὐτός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.12; ἑτέραν ἔδωκεν παντὶ τῷ κόσμῳ ὄψιν “OGI” 458.7 (1st c.BC)}}; ἕ. εὐαγγέλιον {{NT|NT.Gal.1.6: coupled with ἄλλος, χἀτέρους ἄλλους πόνους}} and other **different** toils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 573 (assuming variant), compare “Or.” 345 (uncertain reading); Ῥόδον καὶ ἄλλας ἑτέρας πόλεις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.27; ἕτερον τό τ᾽ ἀλγεῖν καὶ θεωρεῖν ἐστ᾽ ἴσως 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 75.7; ἕτερα φρονῶν καὶ δημηγορῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.17}}: c._genitive_, **other than, different from**, φίλους.. ἑτέρους τῶν νῦν ὄντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44, etc.}}; ἕτερον, ἕτερα ἢ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 345, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2; παρὰ ταῦτα πάντα ἕτερόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 74a; ἕτερα πολιτείας εἴδη παρὰ μοναρχίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294a25, compare 1286b21.}}
**III.2** **other than should be**, euphemistic for κακός, παθεῖν μὲν εὖ, παθεῖν δὲ θάτερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 503; ἀγάθ᾽ ἢ θάτερα, ἵνα μηδὲν εἴπω φλαῦρον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.12: _absolutely_, δαίμων ἕ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.34}}; λέκτρα, συμφοραί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 639 (Lyric poetry), “HF” 1238; ἐὰν τὰ ἕ. ψηφίσωνται οἱ δικασταί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.30}}; πλέον θάτερον ἐποίησαν did more **harm** (than good), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114e, “Euthd.” 280e, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.117 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**IV** Special Phrases:
**IV.1** elliptical, mostly in _dative_ _feminine_,
**IV.1.a** τῇ ἑτέρᾳ (i.e. χειρί), _Epic dialect_ ἑτέρῃ or ἑτέρηφι, **with one hand** (see. entry [near the start]); **with the left hand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.441, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.80, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.45: hence proverbial, οὐ τῇ ἑτέρᾳ ληπτός}} not to be caught with **one** hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 226a; ἐκ δ᾽ ἑτέρης 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1115, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.650 (6th c.AD: Leontius Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV.1.b** θατέρᾳ (i.e. ἡμέρᾳ) **on the morrow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 782, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 449; τῆς ἑτέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44a}}; but τῇ ἑτέρᾳ **on the following** (i.e. **the third) day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.10.}}
**IV.1.c** (i.e. ὁδῷ) **in another** or **a different way**, καὶ τῇδε φῦναι χἀτέρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1444}}; **another way**, τρέπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 812; ἑτέρᾳ πῃ [prev. author] “Eq.” 35}}; τότ᾽ ἄλλοσ᾽.., θατέρᾳ δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 272}}; θατέρᾳ.., θατέρᾳ.. **in one way.., in the other**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 5.16; ἑτέρηφι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 216: _accusative_ ἑτέραν ἐκτρέπεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 5.}}
**IV.2** _adverbial_ with Preps.:
**IV.2.a** ἐπὶ θάτερα **to the one** or **the other side, one** or **the other way**, ἐπὶ μὲν θάτερα.., ἐπὶ θ. δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7}}; τότε μὲν ἐπὶ θάτερα, τότε δ᾽ ἐπὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 259c: also with another _preposition_, ἐς τὰ ἐπὶ θάτερα}} **to** or **on the other side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87}}; ἐκ τοῦ ἐπὶ θάτερα **from the other side**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.37}}; ἐκ μὲν τοῦ ἐπὶ θ., ἐκ δὲ τοῦ ἐπὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314e: with _genitive_, ἐς τὰ ἐπὶ θ. τοῦ ποταμοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.84; εἰς τἀπὶ θ. τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.7; τὸ ἐπὶ θάτερον τῆς ῥινός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 35.}}
**IV.2.b** κατὰ θάτερα **on the one** or **other side**, κατὰ θ. ἀστός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.30; ψόφου κατὰ θ. προσπεσόντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 51, etc.}}; but καθ᾽ ἕτερα **at other points**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42.}}
**V** _adverb_ **ἑτέρως** **in one** or **the other way**, opposed to ἀμφοτέρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181e}}; ἑ. τε καὶ ἑ., ={ἀμφοτέρως}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 235a}}; τοῦ σκέλους ἑ. ἔχειν, ={ἑτεροσκελὴς εἶναι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.39.}}
**V.2** **differently**, rarely in Poetry, οὐχ ἑ. τις ἐρεῖ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 10.3}}; ἑ. ἔχειν to be **different**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 371: frequently in Prose, ὡς ἑ}}. **in the other way** (compare ὡς), ἢν ἡ ἑτέρη γνάθος ἐκστῇ ὡς ἑ. χρὴ τὴν ἐπίδεσιν ἄγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 266a, etc.}}; ἐάν τε καλῶς, ἐάν θ᾽ ὡς ἑ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.85, compare 212}}: with _genitive_, **differently from**, ἑ. πως τῶν εἰωθότων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295d}}; ἑ. ἤπερ.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.28.}}
**V.3** **otherwise than should be, badly, wrongly**, once in {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἑ. ἐβόλοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.234; εἰ καὶ ἑ. τοῦτο ἀπέβη “SIG” 851.10 (Marc.Aur.); εἴ τι ἑ. φρονεῖτε NT.Phil.3.15.}}

2088:
Included with: ** ἕτερος**, α, ον,
only _Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ with **ἕ-**, _Doric dialect_ **ἅτερος** [ᾰ] {{Refs|“IG” 4.914.9 (Epid.), etc. (and _Attic dialect_ in crasis, see.below)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **ἄτερος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41.5, etc.}}:— but **ἅτερος** [ᾱ], _Attic dialect_ crasis for ὁ ἅτερος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 14.23 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, +others}}, _Ionic dialect_ **οὕτερος** (from ὁ ἕτ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ **ὥτερος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.36}}; _neuter_ θάτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 344, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.7, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ τοὔτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32: _plural_ ἅτεροι, for οἱ ἅτεροι, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1255a20; θάτερα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 345, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, etc.; _genitive_ θατέρου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 597, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ τοὐτέρου {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.113}}, _Doric dialect_ θατέρω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 94a, θωτέρω 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 71 (uncertain reading); _dative_ θατέρῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 778; _feminine_ _nominative_ ἡτέρα “IG” 22.1498.76, 1615.14,87 (4th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 497, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 85, 90 codices, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 82; _dative_ θητέρᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 782, “Tr.” 272, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 894, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1365, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus [prev. cited] (in Mss. sometimes θατέρᾳ)}}, _Ionic dialect_ τἠτέρῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 5.2.}} —Later _masculine_ and _feminine_ θάτερος, θατέρα, even with the _Article_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 846, Chrysipp. cited in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 82, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 590, 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Cynegirus” 4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Mort.” 26.1 (condemned in “Pseudol.” 29), “Geoponica” 14.20.2, etc.; τῶν θατέρων 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.83 P.}}; θάτερον _accusative_ _singular_ _masculine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” [849].}}
**I** **one** or **the other of two**, usually with _Article_ except in Poets; frequently of natural pairs, σκαιῇ (i.e. χειρὶ) ἔγχος ἔχων, ἑτέρηφι δὲ λάζετο πέτρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.734}}; τῇ ἑτέρῃ μὲν.. τῇ δ᾽ ἑτέρῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. 14.272, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.11}}; χειρὶ ἑτέρῃ with **one** hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.452, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.171 (but χεὶρ ἑτέρη}} commonly of the **left** hand, see infr. IV.I); ἑτέροιο διὰ κροτάφοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.502; χωλὸς δ᾽ ἕτερον πόδα 2.217, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 162, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.82; ἀμφότεραι αἱ γνάθοι, ἢ ἡ ἑτέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.9; ἐκκοπεὶς τὸν ἕ. τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.23; εἰς γόνυ θάτερον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.20}}; of pairs in general, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.258, etc.}}; τὴν ἑ. πύλην **one of the** two gates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156; ὁ ἕ. τῶν στρατηγῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43; τὸ ἕ. τοῖν δυοῖν τειχοῖν [prev. author] 7.24}}: frequently of alternatives presented, τῶνδε τὰ ἕ. ποιέειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.126}}; ἑλοῦ γε θάτερ᾽, ἢ.. ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 345}}; τοῖνδ᾽ ἑλοῦ δυοῖν πότμοιν τὸν ἕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 952; δυοῖν ἀγαθοῖν τοῦ ἑτέρου τεύξεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28}}; δυοῖν θάτερα, ἢ.. ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187c; ὅταν δυοῖν καλοῖν θάτερον κάλλιον ᾖ, ἢ τῷ ἑτέρῳ τούτοιν ἢ ἀμφοτέροις ὑπερβάλλον κάλλιόν ἐστιν [prev. author] “Grg.” 475a}}: in _plural_, **one of two parties** or **sets**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.258}}; τῶν ἕτεροί γε παῖδα κλαύσονται **one set** of parents, either mine or thine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.210; δώῃ δ᾽ ἑτέροισί γε νίκην 7.292; ἑτέροισι δὲ κῦδος ἔδωκαν 13.303: frequently with _negative_, οὐδ᾽ ἕτεροι 11.71.}}
**I.2** in double clauses ἕτερος (in Prose always ὁ ἕτερος) is generally repeated; ἑ. μὲν δουρὶ.., τῷ δ᾽ ἑ. {{Refs|21.164}}; τὸν ἕ., ἕ. δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.266; ἕ. λευκόν, ἑτέρην δὲ μέλαιναν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.103, etc.: but sometimes omitted in one clause, [ἕτερος μὲν] κακῶν, ἕ. δὲ ἐάων 24.528, compare 7.420, “IG” 22.1388.46 (probably), etc.; ἡ μὲν.., ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.149, “IG” 12.76.50}}; ἕ..., ὁ δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.374; answered by ἄλλος, ἕτερον μὲν κεύθῃ ἐνὶ φρεσίν, ἄλλο δὲ εἴπῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.313, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.123}}; reversely ἄλλῳ ὀρχηστύν, ἑτέρῳ κίθαριν [ἔδωκε] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.731, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439b, “Tht.” 184e}}; τότε μὲν ἕτερα.., τότε δὲ ἄλλα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.116e}}; ὁ ἕτερος.., ὁ λοιπός.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.23}}; ἕτερα.., τὰ δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1454 (Lyric poetry); later μίαν μὲν.. ἑτέραν δέ 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 172.5; τὴν μίαν.. τὴν δ᾽ ἑτέρην “Anthologia Graeca” 9.680.}}
**I.3** repeated in the same clause, ἐξ ἑτέρων ἕτερ᾽ ἐστίν **one** building follows on **another**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.266}}; [ἀ] δ᾽ ἀτέρα τὰν ἀτέραν κύλιξ ὠθήτω let **one** cup push on **the other**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 41.5}}; ἢ θάτερον δεῖ δυστυχεῖν ἢ θάτερον **one party** or **the other**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” [849]; ἕτεροι ἑτέρων ἠξίωσαν ἄρχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; ἕτερος ἀφ᾽ ἑτέρου θεραπείας ἀναπιμπλάμενοι ἔθνῃσκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 51; εἴ τίς τι ἕτερος ἑτέρου προφέρει [prev. author] 7.64; ξυμμειγνυμένων ἑτέρων ἑτέροις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 701}}; συμφορὰ ἑτέρα ἑτέρους πιέζει **one** calamity oppresses **one, another others**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 893 (Lyric poetry); ἑτέρᾳ δ᾽ ἕτερος ἕτερον ὄλβῳ καὶ δυνάμει παρῆλθεν [prev. author] “Ba.” 905, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 231 (_anapaest meter_); ἄλλη δ᾽ εἰς ἑτέρην ὀλυφύρετο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.250.}}
**I.4** ={δεύτερος}, **second**, ἡ μὲν.., ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρη.., ἡ δὲ τρίτη.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.352f, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.179, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.22}}; ἡ ἑ. πρότασις the **minor** premiss, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143b3: without _Article_, ἕ. τέρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57}}; προσαγορεύεις αὐτὰ ἑτέρῳ ὀνόματι you call them further by a **new** name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 13a; cf. IV. 1b.}}
**I.4.b** with Pronouns of quantity, ordinals, etc., τόσσοι δ᾽ αὖθ᾽ ἕτεροι ποταμοί as many **more**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 367}}; ἕτερον τοσοῦτον as much **again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}; ἑτέρου τοσούτου χρόνου for as long **again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.153}}; ἕ. τοιαῦτα **other things** of like kind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 191; ἑτέρων τοιῶνδε (i.e. ἀνθρώπων) ἄρχεις [prev. work] 207}}; τῷ αὐτῷ τρόπῳ.. τῷ ἑτέρῳ in the same way **over again**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.127; ἄλλα τε τοιαῦθ᾽ ἕτερα μυρία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 333.4}}; χιλίας ἑτέρας [δραχμάς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.6}}; δεύτερον, τρίτον ἕ. δικαστήριον, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.71,74}}; ἕ. ἐγώ, of a friend, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.35 P.}}; ἕτεροι αὐτοί **second** selves, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161b28; εὕρηκε τὸν ἕ., τὸν σέ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 474.}}
**II** without _Article_, **another**, of many, with a sense of **difference**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.306, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 422, “Lys.” 66, etc.; ἕ.αὖ τις [prev. author] “Eq.” 949; ἕ. αὖ [prev. author] “Pax” 295, etc.; ἕτερα ἄττα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 188b; repeated ἑτέραν χἀτέραν τρικυμίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 536.8: with _negative_, οἷα οὐχ ἕτερα [ἐγένετο]}} such as none **like them** had happened, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23; ναυμαχία.. οἵα οὐχ ἑτέρα τῶν προτέρων [prev. author] 7.70; οὐδεμιᾶς ἥσσων μᾶλλον ἑτέρας [prev. work] 29 (assuming variant)}}; οὐχ ἕτερον ἀλλά.. **none other than**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.671b, compare “UPZ” 71.9 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.b** οἱ ἕ. **the rest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.169.}}
**II.c** ὁ ἑ. '**one's neighbour**', ἀγαπᾶν τὸν ἕ. {{NT|NT.Rom.13.6, cf. NT.Gal.6.4.}}
**III** **of another kind, different**, ἕ. δέ με θυμὸς ἔρυκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.302}}; τὸ μὲν ἕ., τὸ δὲ ἕ., i.e. they are **different**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97d, compare “R.” 346a; ἕ. τε καὶ ἀνόμοιον [prev. author] “Smp.” 186b; τὸ δὲ ταὐτὸν ἕ. ἀποφαίνειν καὶ τὸ θάτερον ταὐτόν [prev. author] “Sph.” 259d; ἕ. ἤδη ἦν καὶ οὐχ ὁ αὐτός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.12; ἑτέραν ἔδωκεν παντὶ τῷ κόσμῳ ὄψιν “OGI” 458.7 (1st c.BC)}}; ἕ. εὐαγγέλιον {{NT|NT.Gal.1.6: coupled with ἄλλος, χἀτέρους ἄλλους πόνους}} and other **different** toils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 573 (assuming variant), compare “Or.” 345 (uncertain reading); Ῥόδον καὶ ἄλλας ἑτέρας πόλεις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.27; ἕτερον τό τ᾽ ἀλγεῖν καὶ θεωρεῖν ἐστ᾽ ἴσως 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 75.7; ἕτερα φρονῶν καὶ δημηγορῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.17}}: c._genitive_, **other than, different from**, φίλους.. ἑτέρους τῶν νῦν ὄντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44, etc.}}; ἕτερον, ἕτερα ἢ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 345, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2; παρὰ ταῦτα πάντα ἕτερόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 74a; ἕτερα πολιτείας εἴδη παρὰ μοναρχίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294a25, compare 1286b21.}}
**III.2** **other than should be**, euphemistic for κακός, παθεῖν μὲν εὖ, παθεῖν δὲ θάτερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 503; ἀγάθ᾽ ἢ θάτερα, ἵνα μηδὲν εἴπω φλαῦρον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.12: _absolutely_, δαίμων ἕ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.34}}; λέκτρα, συμφοραί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 639 (Lyric poetry), “HF” 1238; ἐὰν τὰ ἕ. ψηφίσωνται οἱ δικασταί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.30}}; πλέον θάτερον ἐποίησαν did more **harm** (than good), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114e, “Euthd.” 280e, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.117 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**IV** Special Phrases:
**IV.1** elliptical, mostly in _dative_ _feminine_,
**IV.1.a** τῇ ἑτέρᾳ (i.e. χειρί), _Epic dialect_ ἑτέρῃ or ἑτέρηφι, **with one hand** (see. entry [near the start]); **with the left hand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.441, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.80, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.45: hence proverbial, οὐ τῇ ἑτέρᾳ ληπτός}} not to be caught with **one** hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 226a; ἐκ δ᾽ ἑτέρης 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1115, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.650 (6th c.AD: Leontius Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV.1.b** θατέρᾳ (i.e. ἡμέρᾳ) **on the morrow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 782, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 449; τῆς ἑτέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44a}}; but τῇ ἑτέρᾳ **on the following** (i.e. **the third) day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.10.}}
**IV.1.c** (i.e. ὁδῷ) **in another** or **a different way**, καὶ τῇδε φῦναι χἀτέρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1444}}; **another way**, τρέπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 812; ἑτέρᾳ πῃ [prev. author] “Eq.” 35}}; τότ᾽ ἄλλοσ᾽.., θατέρᾳ δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 272}}; θατέρᾳ.., θατέρᾳ.. **in one way.., in the other**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 5.16; ἑτέρηφι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 216: _accusative_ ἑτέραν ἐκτρέπεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 5.}}
**IV.2** _adverbial_ with Preps.:
**IV.2.a** ἐπὶ θάτερα **to the one** or **the other side, one** or **the other way**, ἐπὶ μὲν θάτερα.., ἐπὶ θ. δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7}}; τότε μὲν ἐπὶ θάτερα, τότε δ᾽ ἐπὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 259c: also with another _preposition_, ἐς τὰ ἐπὶ θάτερα}} **to** or **on the other side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87}}; ἐκ τοῦ ἐπὶ θάτερα **from the other side**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.37}}; ἐκ μὲν τοῦ ἐπὶ θ., ἐκ δὲ τοῦ ἐπὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314e: with _genitive_, ἐς τὰ ἐπὶ θ. τοῦ ποταμοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.84; εἰς τἀπὶ θ. τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.7; τὸ ἐπὶ θάτερον τῆς ῥινός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 35.}}
**IV.2.b** κατὰ θάτερα **on the one** or **other side**, κατὰ θ. ἀστός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.30; ψόφου κατὰ θ. προσπεσόντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 51, etc.}}; but καθ᾽ ἕτερα **at other points**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42.}}
**V** _adverb_ **ἑτέρως** **in one** or **the other way**, opposed to ἀμφοτέρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181e}}; ἑ. τε καὶ ἑ., ={ἀμφοτέρως}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 235a}}; τοῦ σκέλους ἑ. ἔχειν, ={ἑτεροσκελὴς εἶναι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.39.}}
**V.2** **differently**, rarely in Poetry, οὐχ ἑ. τις ἐρεῖ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 10.3}}; ἑ. ἔχειν to be **different**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 371: frequently in Prose, ὡς ἑ}}. **in the other way** (compare ὡς), ἢν ἡ ἑτέρη γνάθος ἐκστῇ ὡς ἑ. χρὴ τὴν ἐπίδεσιν ἄγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 266a, etc.}}; ἐάν τε καλῶς, ἐάν θ᾽ ὡς ἑ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.85, compare 212}}: with _genitive_, **differently from**, ἑ. πως τῶν εἰωθότων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295d}}; ἑ. ἤπερ.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.28.}}
**V.3** **otherwise than should be, badly, wrongly**, once in {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἑ. ἐβόλοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.234; εἰ καὶ ἑ. τοῦτο ἀπέβη “SIG” 851.10 (Marc.Aur.); εἴ τι ἑ. φρονεῖτε NT.Phil.3.15.}}

2089:
** ἔτι** [ῐ],
_adverb_:
**I** of Time,
**I.1** of the Present, **yet, still**, ἔ. μοι μένος ἔμπεδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.254; ἔ. τυτθὸν ἐόντα 6.222; εἰ Ζεὺς ἔ. Ζεύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 623}}; ἔτ᾽ ἐκ βρέφεος **ever** since babyhood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.567 (Antip.); ἔ. καὶ νῦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.455}}; ἔ. καὶ ἐκ παρόντων variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77; ἔτ᾽ ἂν ἐκ τῶνδε θεὸς χρῄζων θείη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 340; ἔ. καὶ νυνί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215d; νῦν ἔ. ζεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 708 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 818.}}
**I.2** of the Past, mostly with _imperfect_, ἀήθεσσον γὰρ ἔτ᾽ αὐτῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.493, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.102, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111, etc.}}: with _aorist_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310c, etc.}}; ἔ. πρότερον, πρόσθεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242d}}: with the sense, **already**, γεγονέναι ἔ. οὐχ ἧττον ἢ εἶναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 93a.}}
**I.3** of the Future, **yet, longer**, ἄλγε᾽ ἔδωκεν.. ἠδ᾽ ἔ. δώσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.96, compare 5.465: with _optative_, ἔ... φιλέοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.305: with _imperative_, μή τις ἔ... ἔστω 2.230}}; **hereafter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 907, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 66, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 758 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**I.4** with a _negative_, no **longer**, οὐδὲ.. ἔ. παρέμειναν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.46}}; see at {οὐκέτι},{μηκέτι}.
**II** of Degree, **still, besides**, ἐς δεκάτην γενεὴν ἕτερόν γ᾽ ἔ. βόσκοι **another** (and another and so on), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.325; ἔτ᾽ ἄλλο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 157, compare 11.6.411, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.623, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 218, etc.}}; τίν᾽ οὖν ἔτ᾽ ἄλλον..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 114}}; πρὸς τοῖσδ᾽ ἔ., πρὸς τούτοις ἔ. (compare προσέτι), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1339, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 720 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἔ. τε and **besides, nay more**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a}}; ἔ. τοσόνδε this **further** point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 184b; ἔ. δὲ καί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, etc.}}; πρῶτον μὲν.., ἔπειτα δὲ.., ἔ. δὲ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.13}}; ἔ. καί alone, τά τε εἴδωλα, ἔ. καὶ τὰ γεγραμμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239d; ἔτι καὶ ἔ. ἀεί “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 30.}}
**II.2** frequently to strengthen a _comparative_, ἔ. μᾶλλον **yet** more, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.97, 362; μᾶλλον ἔ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.22}}; ἔ. καὶ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.57; καὶ ἔ. καὶ μᾶλλον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.24; ἔ. πλέον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; πλέον ἔ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80; παῖς τε κἄτι τοῦδ᾽ ἀνούστερος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 987; πότμῳ τῷ νῦν.. κἄτι τοῦδ᾽ ἐχθίονι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 272, compare “El.” 559, 1189.}}
**II.3** with the Posit., ἔ. ἄνω **yet** higher up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.9; ἔ. μάλα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 53, 462, “Ra.” 864}}. (Sanskrit _áti_ 'beyond', Latin _et_, Gothic _ip_ 'but', 'however'.)

2090:
** ἑτοιμ-άζω**,
_perfect_ ἡτοίμακα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.72.6:}}_perfect_ _passive_ ἡτοίμασμαι both in _middle_ and _passive_ sense see below): (ἑτοῖμος):—**get ready, prepare**, ἐμοὶ γέρας αὐτίχ᾽ ἑτοιμάσατ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.118}}; [νέας] {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95; στρατιώτας NT.Act.23.23; ὁδόν LXX.Isa.40.3, +others; ἔγκλημα μικρὸν αἰτίαν τε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 361; δῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 364; βουλήν [prev. author] “Heracl.” 472}}; δάκρυα δ᾽ ἑτοιμάζουσι **to those furnishing** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 454; ἀργύριον ῥητόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.7, etc.}}; ἑαυτὸν ἵνα.. {{NT|NT.Rev.8.6.}}
**II** _middle_, **cause to be prepared**, ὄφρ᾽ ἱρὸν ἑτοιμασσαίατ᾽ Ἀθήνῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.571; ἑτοιμάσσαντο δὲ ταύρους 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.184, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.24; ἑτοιμασάμενος ἃ δεῖ “Inscription Prien.” 55.34 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** with _perfect_ _passive_ ἡτοίμασμαι, **prepare for oneself**, τἄλλα ἡτοιμάζετο **made his** other **arrangements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77; ὅπως ἑτοιμάσαιντο τιμωρίαν [prev. author] 1.58; πλείονα ἡτοιμασμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.5; τροφὴν ἡτοιμασμένοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.209; τὰ πρὸς τὸν βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 30, compare 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 53.}}
**II.3** **prepare oneself, make oneself ready**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 8; πρὸς τὴν χειμασίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.105.11.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be prepared**, ἔλεγε ἡτοιμάσθαι **that preparations had been made**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.64, compare 7.62, etc.}}; ἑ. τι **to be prepared with**.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.30.7.}}

2091:
** ἑτοιμ-ᾰσία**, ἡ,
**readiness**, πρὸς τὰς ὑπουργίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 12}}; εἰς ἑ. ὑμῶν παρέχειν to place at your **disposal**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 10.1.2.}}
**II** **preparation**, ἀρμένων {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 21.1, cf. LXX.Psa.9.38 (10.17)}}; τροφῆς{{LXX|LXX.Wis.13.12}}; **equipment**, ἐν -ασίᾳ εὐαγγελίου {{NT|NT.Eph.6.15.}}

2092:
** ἑτοῖμος**, ον,
also _feminine_ ἑτοίμη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.425, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 66, -μᾱ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1079 (Lyric poetry), etc.:—in 5th c.BC and later ἕτοιμος}}, η, ον, or ος, ον, cf. Hdn.Gr. {{Refs|2.938}}:—**at hand, ready, prepared**, ὀνείαθ᾽ ἑτοῖμα προκείμενα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.453, etc.; τὶν δ᾽ αἶνος ἑτοῖμος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.12; [τὰ κρέα] εἶχε ἕτοιμα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, compare 3.123; ἑτοιμοτάταν ἐπὶ δαῖτα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.63, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 357 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἕ. χρήματα money **in hand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.31; ἐξ ἑ.}}**in ready money,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 2106.23 (4th c.AD)}}; ἕ. ἀεὶ παρακείμενον ἐκμαγεῖον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72c}}; ἕ. ποιήσασθαι to make **ready**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11; ὡς ἑτοῖμα ἦν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3; ἐπειδὴ αὐτῷ ἑ. ἦν [prev. work] 98}}; ἐξ ἑτοίμου **at once and without hesitation, immediately, offhand**, ἐξ ἑ. λαμβάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.96; ἐξ ἑ. ὑπακούειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.3; ἐξ ἑτοιμοτάτου διώκειν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.3.57; ἐξ ἑ. φίλον εἶναι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.16}}; γίνεται ταῦτα ἐξ -οτάτου are **most likely to attack**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 24; ἐν ἑτοίμῳ ἐστί 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U., compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.61; ἐν ἑ. ἔχειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.34.2, NT.2Cor.10.6, etc.}}; ἑτοιμότερα γέλωτος λίβη tears **that came more readily than**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 448}}; τὰ ἑ. **that which is ready to hand**, ἐπὶ τὰ ἑ. μᾶλλον τρέπονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20}}; τὰ ἑ. βλάψαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 70; τοῖς ἑ. περὶ τῶν ἀφανῶν.. κινδυνεύειν [prev. author] 6.9.}}
**2** of the future, **sure to come, certain**, αὐτίκα γάρ τοι ἔπειτα μεθ᾽ Ἕκτορα πότμος ἑ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.96; χώλωσις ἑτοίμη τοῖσι περιγινομένοισι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 66}}; also, **easy to be done, feasible**, ἐπεὶ οὔ σφισιν ἥδε γ᾽ ἑτοίμη (i.e. μῆτις) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.425}}; ἕ. [ἐστι] τὸ διαφθαρῆναι **imminent**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.706c}}: with _infinitive_, ἕ. μᾶλλόν [ἐστι] ἀπεχθάνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567a, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 86}}; οὐ γάρ τι ἕ. μεταπεῖσαι it is not **easy**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.23.6.}}
**3** of the past, **carried into effect, realized**, ταῦτα ἑ. τετεύχαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.53}}; ἠδ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἑτοῖμα τέτυκτο and this promise has been **made good**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.384.}}
