2095:
** εὖ**,
_Epic dialect_ also ἐΰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.302, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 200.20:}} _adverb_ (properly _neuter_ of ἐΰς):—**well**, opposed to κακῶς (as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.63), 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**I** of knowledge or action, **well, thoroughly, competently**, εὖ μέν τις δόρυ θηξάσθω, εὖ δ᾽ ἀσπίδα θέσθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.382; εὖ καὶ ἐπισταμένως κέασαν ξύλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.161; τὴν πόλιν κοσμέων καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; τὸ πρᾶγμα βασανίσας καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 307b, etc.}}; τόξων ἐῢ εἰδώς **cunning** with the bow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.718, etc.; εὖ τόδ᾽ ἴσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 173 (troch.); εὖ γὰρ σαφῶς τόδ᾽ ἴστε [prev. work] 784}}; εὖ οἶδ᾽ ὅτι parenthetic in colloquial speech, σὺ γὰρ εὖ οἶδ᾽ ὅτι οὐ πράγματ᾽ ᾄσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1296, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.2, etc.}}; εὖ οἶδα, in answers, Dioxipp.4; εὖ μήδεο consider **well**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.360}}; εὖ λέγεις **well** spoken ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.2, etc.: with λέγω omitted, οὐδὲ τοῦτ᾽ εὖ Ἐρατοσθένης 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.1.}}
**I.2** **morally well, kindly**, εὖ ἔρδειν, ={εὐεργετεῖν}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.650}}; εὖ εἰπεῖν τινα to speak **well** of him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.302; εὖ δρᾶν εὖ παθών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 672, etc.}}
**I.3** with passive or intransitive Verbs, **fortunately, happily, in good case**, εὖ ζώουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.79}}; εὖ οἴκαδ᾽ ἱκέσθαι **safely**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.19, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.188; τοῦ βίου εὖ ἥκειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30}}; εὖ φρονῶν in one's **right** mind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 387, etc. (but εὖ φρονεῖν εἴς τινας, τὰ σά}}, to be **well-disposed** towards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.4, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 491)}}; standing last for emphasis, ἄνδρες γεγονότες εὖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.134; νόμους μὴ λύειν ἔχοντας εὖ [prev. author] 3.82; τελευτήσει τὸν βίον εὖ [prev. author] 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1124b13, etc.}}: separated from its Verb, εὖ πρᾶγμα συντεθέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.144.}}
**II** coupled with other Adverbs, especially when qualifying nouns, adjectives, and adverbs, εὖ μάλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.96, etc.; ἡ ἀορτὴ εὖ μάλα κοίλη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 514b22; εὖ μάλα πᾶσαι “hymnus ad Apollinem” 171; εὖ μάλα πολλά 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 35; εὖ μάλα πρεσβύτης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4a; μάλα εὖ καὶ κομψῶς [prev. author] “Sph.” 236d; εὖ καὶ μάλα [prev. author] “Smp.” 194a (sed compare “CQ” 15.4); κάρτα εὖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.150}}; εὖ.. πάνυ or πάνυ εὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 198, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 80b; εὖ σφόδρα 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus 8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 75.4}}; εὖ κἀνδρικῶς, εὖ κἀνδρείως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 379 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 656}}; καλῶστεκαὶ εὖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(see.above 1.1); εὖ τε καὶ καλῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 503d.}}
**III** as _substantive_, τὸ εὖ the **right**, the **good cause**, τὸ δ᾽ εὖ νικάτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 121; τὸ γὰρ εὖ μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 661; the “Good”, final cause, τὸ εὖ τεκταινόμενος ἐν πᾶσιν τοῖς γιγνομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68e; τοῦ εὖ ἕνεκα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 437a1, compare [same work] “Metaphorical” 1092b26}}: in Art, **perfection, the ideal**, τὸ εὖ διὰ πολλῶν ἀριθμῶν γίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Polyclitus Philosophus 2.}}
**IV** as the Predicate of a _proposition_, τί τῶνδ᾽ ε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 338 (Lyric poetry), compare 116}}; εὖ εἴη may it be **well**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 216 (Lyric poetry)}}; εὐορκεῦντι μέμ μοι εὖ εἶμεν or εἴη, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 953.9 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC), “PEleph.” 23.19 (3rd c.BC)}}; εὖ σοι γένοιτο **well** be with thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 627, compare “fragment” 707.}}
**V** Interjection, **well done**! to cheer on dogs, εὖ κύνες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.20}}; **ahoy! ho!** {{Refs|Lyric poetry Alex.Adesp. 20.11}}; compare εὖγε.
**VI** in Compds., implying **abundance** (εὐανδρία), **prosperity** (εὐδαίμων, opposed to κακοδαίμων), **ease** (εὔβατος, opposed to δύσβατος): compounded only with Nouns and _adjectives_ (hence εὖ πάσχω, εὖ ποιέω are better written divisim, but εὐποιητικός implies εὐποιέω: see at {ἀντευποιέω}); εὐδοκέω is exceptional. (Replaced by καλῶς in later Gr., except in set phrases.)

2097:
** εὐαγγελ-ίζομαι**,
_imperfect_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.19.5}}: _future_ _participle_ -ιούμενος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.4.2, 18.6.10, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 34}}: _aorist_ (see. below):—_active_, only in later Gr., {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.31.9, NT.Rev.10.7, “PGiss.” 27.6 (2nd c.AD):}}_pluperfect_ εὐηγγελίκειν uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 61.13}}: (εὐάγγελος):— **bring good news, announce them**, λόγους ἀγαθοὺς φέρων εὐαγγελίσασθαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 643, compare 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.323; τὴν εὐτοκίαν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.70; εὐτυχίας τῇ πατρίδι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 18; πρός σε ταῦτα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 83; also τινά τι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.10, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.12, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.10}}; εὐ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.7}}; τινι ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 31}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 22:}} —_active_, εὐ. τὰ τῆς νίκης “PGiss.” [same place]; τισιν ὡς.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.7}}:—_passive_, **receive good tidings**, ἐν ᾗ -ίσθη ἡ πόλις ἡμέρᾳ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“AJA” 18.323 (Sardes, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** **preach** or **proclaim as glad tidings**, τὴν βασιλείαν τοῦ Θεοῦ {{NT|NT.Luke.4.43, etc.; εἰρήνην ὑμῖν NT.Eph.2.17, etc.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **proclaim glad tidings**, πτωχοῖς {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.61.1, cf. NT.Luke.4.18, etc.}}: with _accusative_, **preach the glad tidings of the gospel to**, τὸν λαόν{{NT|[prev. work] 3.18; κώμας τῶν Σαμαρειτῶν NT.Act.8.25}}:—so in _active_, {{NT|NT.Rev.10.7}}; τινι {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.31.9}}:—_passive_, **have the gospel preached to** one, {{NT|NT.Matt.11.5, NT.Heb.4.2, 6}}; also of the gospel, **to be preached,** {{NT|NT.Luke.16.16, NT.Gal.1.11.}}

2098:
** εὐαγγέλ-ιον**, τό,
**reward of good tidings**, given to the messenger, εὐαγγέλιον δέ μοι ἔστω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.152; οὐ.. εὐ. τόδε τείσω [prev. work] 166}}; ἀπολήψῃ τὸ εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 17: in _Attic dialect_ always in _plural_, εὐαγγέλια θύειν}} to make a thank-offering for **good-tidings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 415; εὐ. θύειν ἑκατὸν βοῦς τῇ θεῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 656}}; ἐβουθύτει ὡς εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.14; εὐαγγελίων θυσίαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.160}}; εὐ. στεφανοῦν, ἀναδῆσαί τινα, to crown one for **good news brought**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 647, “Pl.” 765; ἐστεφανωμένη ἐπ᾽ εὐαγγελίοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 11, compare “Supp.epigram” 1.362.7 (Samos, 4th c.BC).}}
**II** **good tidings, good news**, in _plural_, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.4.10, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 2.3.1, 13.40.1, “Inscription Prien.” 105.40 (1st c.BC): _sing._, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.17.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 26, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.93, “Sammelb.” 421 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** in Christian sense, **the gospel,** {{NT|NT.Gal.1.11, etc.}}

2099:
** εὐαγγελ-ιστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**bringer of good tidings**: hence, **evangelist, preacher of the gospel,** {{NT|NT.Acts.21.8.}}
**II** **proclaimer of oracular messages,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(1).675 (from Rhodes).}}
